00:11:57.739 --> 00:12:02.979
Otherwise, let's go ahead and continue talking about architectures and distributed systems.

00:12:03.879 --> 00:12:11.980
So last week, we started talking about logical architectures, and we talked first about layered architectures.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:15.240
We described various ways you can organize layered architectures.

00:12:15.399 --> 00:12:22.200
As an example, we used application layering, which is a very popular architecture for organizing applications at a logical level.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.980
After that, we talked about object-based styles or service-oriented architectures.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:33.240
pictures. So here instead of organize the application as layers, we organize them as

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:39.560
independent components or objects, and each object has its own interface, each object has its own

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:46.039
state and methods that it runs, and then the objects, they can use each other's services by

00:12:46.039 --> 00:12:51.899
making calls to their interfaces. This follows the object-oriented, let's say, programming

00:12:51.899 --> 00:12:57.039
paradigm or lessons learned from the object-oriented programming paradigm.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.879
And then as an example of a service-oriented architecture, we have, of course, the REST

00:13:01.879 --> 00:13:02.459
architecture.

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:09.279
So REST uses the notion that the distributive system will manage some resources.

00:13:09.559 --> 00:13:13.179
So the components inside the distributive system are just responsible with managing

00:13:13.179 --> 00:13:20.600
resources, which means that in order to operate on these resources, we create a universal

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.800
interface. So the interface

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.759
basically provides

00:13:24.759 --> 00:13:26.540
mechanisms to create new resources,

00:13:26.779 --> 00:13:28.759
to read resources, modify resources,

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:30.680
and delete resources, using

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:32.560
the familiar put, get, delete, and post

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.740
operations that you see in web applications.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:36.720
And then if

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.060
you can build some sort of

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.940
naming schema

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.639
in your system, then you can identify

00:13:42.639 --> 00:13:44.639
a resource uniquely, and then you can

00:13:44.639 --> 00:13:46.940
do operations. The advantage

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.740
of this approach over the

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:57.560
object-oriented interface is that when you submit an operation, you do one of

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:03.060
those four operations of a unique identifier and then inside the message of

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:09.360
this request you include the details of whatever operation you do on the

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:14.639
object. So if you need to add extra information to the object or if you need

00:14:14.639 --> 00:14:21.319
attributes, for example, for objects, you know, permissions, share settings, you know,

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:23.919
you can do that by adding extra information in the message.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.840
This means that the applications, they just have to process a piece of text and extract

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:34.319
all of the valuable information from it, which allows you to use a universal interface, makes

00:14:34.319 --> 00:14:35.860
it very easy to add new services.

00:14:36.559 --> 00:14:41.240
With the object-oriented interface, right, whenever you change the application programming

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:43.939
interface, you need to change the applications as well.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.020
otherwise they won't be able to compile or run.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:47.100
All right.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:51.380
But, and I think we stop here at the coordination part.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.620
So, so far, when we talked about these logical architectures,

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.420
we didn't make any assumption about, you know,

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.360
the components that are either layers or objects.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.340
When do they communicate with each other?

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.140
Do they have to, you know, when they communicate,

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:09.940
does a component need to know exactly

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:12.840
with which other component does it need to communicate with?

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:20.200
So this falls in the area of coordination between components inside the distributed system.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:25.180
So all of these questions, how do the components have to be up and running at the same time?

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:28.259
Does a component need to know where to find another component?

00:15:28.399 --> 00:15:32.420
These are all valid questions, and there are questions that are in the area of coordination.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:38.540
So it's important to understand the problem of coordination because then it will allow

00:15:38.539 --> 00:15:44.599
to actually define some more interesting, let's say, logical ways for organizing distributed applications.

00:15:45.639 --> 00:15:51.259
So when it comes to coordination, there are two, let's say, factors that matter

00:15:51.259 --> 00:15:55.139
when it comes to how two components can communicate with each other.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.399
So there is a temporal factor.

00:15:57.839 --> 00:16:00.819
So the temporal factor refers to, okay, are the components,

00:16:00.959 --> 00:16:04.819
they have to be up and running at the same time in order for them to be able to communicate?

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:07.760
or can one of the components be offline

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.840
and the communication can still happen?

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:11.940
I'll give some examples of what that means.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:16.640
And then lastly, the referential factor refers to

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.260
does a component need to know exactly

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:21.860
which other component does it need to communicate with

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.640
or can he send a message to

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.160
and any component can pick it up?

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:28.780
We'll see some examples of where this is useful.

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:33.020
So in the book, the authors, they call these

00:16:33.019 --> 00:16:35.039
temporal and referential couplings.

00:16:35.919 --> 00:16:39.299
And you can have two variants of, you know,

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:40.199
temporal couplings.

00:16:40.319 --> 00:16:44.559
So you can have systems that have components

00:16:44.559 --> 00:16:47.399
that are temporally coupled versus temporally decoupled,

00:16:47.399 --> 00:16:50.419
and then components that are referentially coupled

00:16:50.419 --> 00:16:51.399
and referentially decoupled.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:55.299
This creates now four categories of coordination

00:16:55.299 --> 00:16:56.579
in distributive systems.

00:16:57.179 --> 00:16:58.699
So let's start with the first one.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:02.899
So the direct coordination model refers to

00:17:02.899 --> 00:17:06.900
systems where the components are temporally coupled and referentially coupled.

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:11.359
So it means that the components inside the distributive system, and when they communicate

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:15.139
with each other, so temporally coupled means that they have to be up and running at the

00:17:15.139 --> 00:17:20.059
same time, and referentially coupled, you need that component initiates a request.

00:17:20.559 --> 00:17:23.139
It needs to know exactly what is the destination component.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:32.820
So as an example of the direct coordination model, we can use the cell phone system, right?

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:37.820
So for example, if you want to be able to call someone, right, you need to know who you're calling.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.380
You cannot just call somebody in the ETA, right?

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:42.940
You need to have a destination phone number that you're calling.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:45.640
So that's what it means by referentially coupled.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:46.759
You have to have a destination.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:52.740
And then temporary coupled means that both phones have to be up and running and connected to the network for the call to go through.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:58.040
If one of the phones is not connected to the network or is shut off, the call will not go through.

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:01.259
You will get immediately an indication that, hey, you cannot call this number.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:03.120
All right.

00:18:03.759 --> 00:18:11.580
The mailbox coordination model refers to systems where the components are temporally decoupled but referentially coupled.

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:18.220
So, temporally decoupled means that the components do not have to be up and running at the same time.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:22.019
the message will still be sent between them

00:18:22.019 --> 00:18:25.539
but you have to, again, referentially coupled means that a component needs to know

00:18:25.539 --> 00:18:28.100
with which other component does it need to talk to

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.680
so as an example, I'm going to use electronic mail, email

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:34.819
as a way to describe

00:18:34.819 --> 00:18:36.620
an example for this model

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:41.819
so in the email model, the components represent the people that send

00:18:41.819 --> 00:18:43.380
emails between each other

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:48.059
the system is temporally decoupled because I can send an email

00:18:48.059 --> 00:18:51.659
to one of my friends, even though they're not connected to their computer right now.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.119
The computer is offline.

00:18:53.679 --> 00:18:56.559
The email gets sent somewhere in the server where it gets stored.

00:18:56.559 --> 00:18:59.579
So we need some notion of persistency we'll see very shortly.

00:19:00.519 --> 00:19:03.919
But whenever I send an email, I cannot send the email to the ether.

00:19:04.279 --> 00:19:07.259
I need to send an email to a destination.

00:19:07.799 --> 00:19:12.139
The destination could be another email address, could be, for example, an email group.

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:18.120
and the email group will then send an email to each of the members of the group

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:20.180
but in the end we still need a destination.

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:24.300
So this is an example of the mailbox coordination model.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:31.420
Now when we go to systems that are referentially decoupled

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.160
we have two examples here.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:38.740
We have event-based systems and the shared data space model.

00:19:39.339 --> 00:19:44.559
So here, what they both have in common is that they have some sort of event bus

00:19:44.559 --> 00:19:49.680
where the components can actually submit the request directly to the event bus.

00:19:50.259 --> 00:19:54.579
And then there are other components that can capture those requests from the event bus.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:58.900
The beauty of it is that when the components communicate with the event bus,

00:19:59.019 --> 00:20:01.299
they don't have to have a destination set up.

00:20:01.299 --> 00:20:05.700
So that's why the components in these two kinds of coordination models,

00:20:05.819 --> 00:20:07.299
they are referentially decoupled.

00:20:07.299 --> 00:20:13.539
So a component doesn't necessarily have to know with whom does it need to communicate or with whom does it need to send a request.

00:20:14.919 --> 00:20:22.940
Now, the difference between them for temporally coupled means that all of the components have to be up and running at the same time when the communication happens.

00:20:23.059 --> 00:20:25.779
Otherwise, the messages get lost on the event bus.

00:20:26.519 --> 00:20:35.279
And then in the shared data space coordination model, we have some sort of persistency component that is part of the event bus that can store those messages

00:20:35.279 --> 00:20:37.899
until they're ready to be picked up by other systems.

00:20:38.039 --> 00:20:41.720
So the components, they can go offline and online whenever they want.

00:20:43.819 --> 00:20:47.480
Now, by convention, when we talk about event-based systems,

00:20:48.859 --> 00:20:54.180
the components, they can perform two kinds of operations on the event bus.

00:20:54.399 --> 00:20:56.700
They can publish information on the event bus,

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.940
or they can subscribe to receive information from the event bus.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:04.940
And then when they subscribe, there are two ways that they can receive information.

00:21:05.279 --> 00:21:08.660
the components can wait until the information is available on the event bus.

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:12.960
Or they can tell the event bus, hey, you know, I'm interested in data.

00:21:13.259 --> 00:21:15.160
Just notify me when the data is available.

00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:18.539
I don't know if we gave as an example last time,

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:25.440
but the way you can understand this event bus is the same way operating systems

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:30.819
allows processes to read data from a disk, right?

00:21:30.819 --> 00:21:38.899
So if I were to ask you, you know, how does a process read data from the disk from a file?

00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:43.039
What does the process do in order to be able to read data from a disk?

00:21:43.659 --> 00:21:50.000
What does a process do and then what does the operating system do?

00:21:50.039 --> 00:21:54.240
Or what are the two ways that a process can read data from the disk?

00:21:54.240 --> 00:22:04.240
To give you a hint, that the disk is a slower device than the processor itself, right?

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.240
So, there are two things that we can do.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:12.240
I mean, isn't the disk indexed already?

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:15.240
So, the operating system goes to read it.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:19.240
It's the index of what's on there, so it doesn't read all of the information.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:20.240
Correct.

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:24.380
But what I'm more interested in is if the disk is slower than the processor, what does

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:30.480
the process do while it's waiting for the data to come from the disk?

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:31.579
Wait.

00:22:31.579 --> 00:22:32.680
Wait, yes.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:34.640
I was just going to say sleep and wait for it.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:35.960
Sleep and wait, right.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.000
So there are two techniques.

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:40.099
They're called polling and using interrupts.

00:22:40.099 --> 00:22:41.440
These are the two techniques.

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:46.559
So polling means that the process will keep on asking the operating system, hey, is the

00:22:46.559 --> 00:22:47.559
data ready yet?

00:22:47.559 --> 00:22:52.000
So keep in mind that the operating system mediates access to hardware.

00:22:52.179 --> 00:22:55.279
So whenever we want to read some data from the disk and bring it to memory,

00:22:55.599 --> 00:22:57.639
the process itself cannot do that operation.

00:22:57.839 --> 00:23:01.039
It needs to ask the operating system to do the operation on its behalf.

00:23:01.779 --> 00:23:04.299
So it sends a message to the operating system,

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:09.220
and then the operating system will go ahead and perform that operation for that process.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:16.480
So we can do polling where the process keeps on waiting, actively waits.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:21.579
So it keeps on running on the CPU and keeps on asking the operating system, hey, is the data ready yet?

00:23:21.579 --> 00:23:23.099
Is the data ready yet in this memory?

00:23:24.519 --> 00:23:29.120
You can ask it, you know, every instruction or you can put a delay, for example.

00:23:29.279 --> 00:23:33.400
You can wait, you know, a few hundred milliseconds until you check to see if the data is available.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:34.860
Again, it depends on the application.

00:23:35.299 --> 00:23:38.539
Now, this is not a very efficient technique, right?

00:23:38.599 --> 00:23:46.380
Because the processor is wasting time or the process is wasting time on the processor, doing nothing, just waiting for data.

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:50.799
So it might be efficient to actually allow other processes to run at the same time.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:54.839
What about the background processes that are waiting for the processor?

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:59.120
Can they run at this time when the data is being read?

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:03.799
Yeah, so they can run only if that process goes to sleep voluntarily.

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:09.740
So if the process decides to stay on the processor, it will stay for the allotted quota of time.

00:24:10.519 --> 00:24:14.680
By operating systems, they allow each process to run for a very short amount of time.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:17.140
And we'll talk more about it in Chapter 3, right?

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.600
But the other option would be for the process to tell the operating system,

00:24:20.759 --> 00:24:24.240
hey, I want this piece of data, and then voluntarily go to sleep

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:27.799
so that other processes can be scheduled on the process.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:31.400
So then what the operating system does is when the data is available,

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:34.920
it sends a signal to the process to let it know, an interrupter,

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:38.779
let it know, hey, your data is ready, and it wakes up the process, right?

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.259
So the signal is sent to some sort of event bus.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:44.319
So the process becomes a subscriber,

00:24:44.759 --> 00:24:46.579
and the operating system becomes the publisher.

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:49.640
The operating system publishes when the data is available

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:50.579
and sends a notification,

00:24:51.279 --> 00:24:53.420
and then the component can use a subscribe function

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:54.879
to actually grab the data from the map.

00:24:55.379 --> 00:24:56.779
It doesn't really work that way,

00:24:56.779 --> 00:24:58.480
but we can imagine it, you know,

00:24:58.759 --> 00:25:00.859
at least at an abstract level that it works this way.

00:25:02.379 --> 00:25:05.319
All right, so this now allows us to introduce

00:25:05.319 --> 00:25:06.879
these notions here,

00:25:07.039 --> 00:25:08.779
these two coordination models,

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:10.619
allows us to introduce,

00:25:10.619 --> 00:25:12.059
and I'm going to come back to the Linda.

00:25:12.179 --> 00:25:17.919
Topal Spaces allows us to introduce the last logical, let's say, architecture

00:25:17.919 --> 00:25:21.239
that we will cover in this class, which is the publisher-subscriber architecture.

00:25:22.219 --> 00:25:26.179
So in a publisher-subscriber architecture, this is again a logical way of organizing

00:25:26.179 --> 00:25:31.059
the distributed application, where our components can be either publishers or subscribers.

00:25:31.959 --> 00:25:36.879
The subscribers, they can subscribe to the services provided by the publishers,

00:25:37.119 --> 00:25:38.879
and the publishers will publish information.

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:43.640
Now, what's very interesting when compared to the other two architectures

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:47.480
is that we need something else to be able to implement the system.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:51.680
We need that event bus, plus maybe the persistency if it's required.

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:58.080
So for this, we need to make use of the middleware layer of distributed systems.

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:01.780
If you remember from the first lecture when we talked about

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:06.520
the distribution transparency goal of distributed systems,

00:26:06.519 --> 00:26:27.480
We said that we want to build a system, that the distributed system is a system that appears to its users as one single coherent system, and it does this to this implementation of this middleware, which is a layer that allows distributed systems to run across multiple computers with different architectures, different operating systems, and it provides one uniform interface.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:31.599
The middleware can also be used to implement this coordination,

00:26:32.039 --> 00:26:38.000
so to implement either the event bus or the shared data space coordination model.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:43.039
So now the middleware is responsible with receiving subscription

00:26:43.039 --> 00:26:46.200
or publish approbations from the components.

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:48.740
Now, why do we need a publisher subscriber?

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:51.680
Or what is the advantage of this architecture?

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:57.440
The advantage is that the components can join the distributed system

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:02.759
or leave at will without requiring us to reboot the entire distributed system.

00:27:03.299 --> 00:27:07.080
If we build it in a layer fashion, if we want to replace one layer with another one,

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:10.120
we will need to, you know, reset the entire distributed system

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:13.600
and replace, you know, all of the function calls to the new layers, right?

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:14.740
This would be very difficult.

00:27:15.299 --> 00:27:18.519
And the object-based as well, depending on which objects get modified,

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:21.000
the interface might change, right?

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:26.539
So this means that we have to, you know, reset parts of the distributed system.

00:27:26.539 --> 00:27:33.099
With the publisher-subscriber model, the components, right, the publisher and the subscribers, they can be implemented in different ways.

00:27:33.099 --> 00:27:40.059
As long as they use the publish and subscribe operations with the middleware, you know, with the event bus, then we're all good.

00:27:40.159 --> 00:27:44.000
They can, you know, leave and join, you know, at will.

00:27:45.119 --> 00:27:49.399
Now, there are a few properties, of course, when it comes to the publisher-subscriber systems.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:59.920
So one of the main challenges of this middleware is that it has to solve the problem of how do we match events to the components that subscribe to those events.

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:09.040
We can say that events can be described by an attribute and value.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:18.019
And then there are two ways we can, let's say, create the subscription model.

00:28:18.019 --> 00:28:22.180
So we can do topic-based subscription or content-based subscription.

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:32.240
So topic-based subscription means that a component that is a subscriber will subscribe to all of the events inside the middleware

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:37.079
that are published by publishers in which the attribute has a specific value.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:39.839
We'll give some examples of what this means.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:45.000
Content-based means that the attribute has a value that sits inside some sort of range.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:51.259
each. So, just to give this a short example, let's say that one day you decide that you

00:28:51.259 --> 00:28:56.440
want to learn more about, you know, buying and selling microstocks in tech companies,

00:28:56.579 --> 00:29:00.559
right? The way you would do this is you would go, you know, create an account, a background

00:29:00.559 --> 00:29:04.079
somewhere, and then download some sort of application that is connected to some sort

00:29:04.079 --> 00:29:08.799
of server that gives you the latest value of microstocks for, you know, various kinds

00:29:08.799 --> 00:29:13.460
of tech companies. With these applications, what you can do is you can also set up push

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:19.460
notifications that whenever there's a change in the stock of a specific company that you're

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:24.240
interested in, you get a notification. So, to know if you want to sell or buy micro stocks from it.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:29.779
Okay, this is an example of a topic-based subscription system, right? So, the server

00:29:29.779 --> 00:29:35.079
that gives you the notifications acts as the middleware. They have, of course, a set of

00:29:35.079 --> 00:29:39.860
publishers, which are the companies that keep track of their stocks or third-party companies

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:43.360
that keep track of the value of the stocks that push the notifications in their server.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:46.880
and then the server would notify all of the applications

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:49.420
that subscribe for those particular companies.

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:51.440
So if I set up my application and say,

00:29:51.559 --> 00:29:54.259
hey, I'd like, again, to receive notifications

00:29:54.259 --> 00:29:58.380
from when Stock Exchange in Intel, NVIDIA, Microsoft, Apple,

00:29:58.799 --> 00:30:00.620
you will receive notifications for them.

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:02.700
So that's an example of topic-based subscription.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:05.100
For content-based subscription,

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:09.279
it means that I'm creating a more complex query there.

00:30:09.759 --> 00:30:12.360
So maybe I want to say that I'm interested actually

00:30:12.359 --> 00:30:14.759
in receiving notifications

00:30:14.759 --> 00:30:17.740
for like the top 10 companies

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:20.099
in which stocks change.

00:30:20.399 --> 00:30:22.439
Top 10 by the value of the stock.

00:30:23.099 --> 00:30:25.379
So this is more of a range query now

00:30:25.379 --> 00:30:26.219
because now you're interested

00:30:26.219 --> 00:30:28.240
in multiple topics, right?

00:30:28.259 --> 00:30:29.399
They can change.

00:30:29.819 --> 00:30:31.119
They're not staying statically.

00:30:31.259 --> 00:30:32.500
So that's an example

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:33.939
of a content-based subscription,

00:30:34.099 --> 00:30:36.000
which typically will be harder to implement.

00:30:36.519 --> 00:30:38.359
But topic-based and content-based,

00:30:38.479 --> 00:30:40.099
they're typically more difficult

00:30:40.099 --> 00:30:40.740
to implement,

00:30:41.259 --> 00:30:44.460
especially in a distributed middleware, right?

00:30:44.480 --> 00:30:46.740
Because you have to keep track of all of the subscribers,

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.259
the kind of information that you need,

00:30:49.339 --> 00:30:52.740
and then whenever you have a piece of data that gets received by the middleware,

00:30:52.799 --> 00:30:57.599
the middleware has to decide to which subscriber to send the data to, right?

00:30:57.599 --> 00:31:00.120
You can use, for example, for topic-based subscriptions,

00:31:00.319 --> 00:31:02.120
you could probably use some sort of hash table.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:07.920
If you keep a hash table where the keys is, let's say, you know, an attribute,

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:13.100
and then the values represent all of the subscribers that subscribe for that particular attribute,

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:15.279
then you could probably solve this problem.

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:17.820
Well, for a content-based subscription, it's more hard.

00:31:18.019 --> 00:31:19.759
A hash table won't be able to help.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:23.720
So just keep in mind that when it comes to the publisher-subscriber systems,

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:30.560
it allows you to, again, build an architecture where the components can join and leave at will.

00:31:32.120 --> 00:31:37.320
However, the main challenge, especially when designing publisher-subscriber middleware,

00:31:37.319 --> 00:31:39.779
is how do you design an efficient system

00:31:39.779 --> 00:31:42.419
that can match the events that come in

00:31:42.419 --> 00:31:43.759
with the proper subscribers.

00:31:44.379 --> 00:31:47.779
So this can sometimes introduce scalability problems, actually.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:50.700
So you might make your application not run very fast

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:54.000
if you choose to use this approach.

00:31:54.579 --> 00:31:56.439
Now, I also have here an example,

00:31:56.439 --> 00:31:58.679
and for some reason the slides are out of order,

00:31:58.899 --> 00:31:59.799
but it doesn't really matter.

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:03.740
So an example of a publisher subscriber system

00:32:03.740 --> 00:32:06.679
can be built using the lean.touple space library.

00:32:06.680 --> 00:32:19.980
So this is a Python library that you can download that implements, that gives you a library that allows you to implement, let's say, publisher subscriber systems.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:22.920
An example that I have here is an example of a blog.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:25.799
So a blog is an example of a publisher subscriber system.

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:31.759
In a blog, you have one or two people or more, one or more people that act as publishers.

00:32:31.759 --> 00:32:33.600
They publish information on the blog.

00:32:34.039 --> 00:32:36.259
And then other people subscribe to that information.

00:32:36.259 --> 00:32:37.640
may read the blog, right?

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:42.960
So, the end of couple spaces allows you to implement this blog in various ways.

00:32:43.079 --> 00:32:46.460
It has three main operations that allows you to interact with the middleware.

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:54.119
So, the end of couple space itself implements the middleware, but it does it on a single

00:32:54.119 --> 00:32:54.720
node system.

00:32:54.839 --> 00:32:58.559
If you want to go across nodes, I think it does have support nowadays, but you need

00:32:58.559 --> 00:33:00.400
to use something else in the example here.

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:12.780
So the three main operations that are supported for the middleware is instead of doing publishing information and subscribing for information, the three main operations are IN, READ, and OUT.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:18.300
So IN will remove a tuple matching a specific template from the middleware.

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:24.019
The READ operation will obtain a copy of a tuple matching a template from the middleware.

00:33:24.480 --> 00:33:29.740
And then OUT will be able to write a tuple T to the tuple space.

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:39.019
So calling out twice in a row leads to storing two copies of a couple, so the in that couple

00:33:39.019 --> 00:33:44.680
space is a multi-set, so it allows multiple copies of the exact same data, and then both

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:50.019
the in and read operations are blocking operations, so the call will block until a matching tuple

00:33:50.019 --> 00:33:52.080
is found or has become a data.

00:33:52.079 --> 00:33:56.399
So this gives you a very simple example of a blog application.

00:33:56.399 --> 00:33:58.399
Sorry.

00:33:58.399 --> 00:34:02.059
A very simple example of a blog application that's with Bob, Alice, and Chuck.

00:34:02.059 --> 00:34:07.539
You know, they all want to create a blog where they can post information and they can read

00:34:07.539 --> 00:34:08.539
information.

00:34:08.539 --> 00:34:12.819
So you can follow if you want to, you know, set this up on your own computer and test

00:34:12.819 --> 00:34:17.759
it, you know, it's a very easy way to build a very simple blog on your system.

00:34:17.760 --> 00:34:20.120
You basically import the Linda library.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:22.300
You connect to the library.

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:24.320
You create a tuple space.

00:34:24.640 --> 00:34:26.440
You have to give it a name when you create it.

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:28.660
You connect them to the tuple space.

00:34:28.660 --> 00:34:32.160
And then you can do these out or read or in operation.

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:35.240
So out, you're writing tuples to the tuple space.

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:42.320
And when you do it, you can choose a template for the information that you're including there.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:47.660
So here, for example, the chosen template is that you want to include the user who posts something

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:52.039
in the blog, the topic and then the actual message that is posted in the blog.

00:34:52.039 --> 00:34:58.259
Whenever you do read operation you specify the user that you want to read a topic from

00:34:58.259 --> 00:35:04.719
and then the topic that you're interested in, and this will return to you some sort of

00:35:04.719 --> 00:35:08.199
piece of text if it's available inside the shared topic space.

00:35:08.199 --> 00:35:12.500
Ok but anyways, this is a very simple example, but the way you can think about this publisher

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:15.280
subscriber systems is similar to a blog.

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:16.780
Okay.

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:24.679
All right, well, these are the three kinds of architectures that you will find out there,

00:35:24.679 --> 00:35:27.300
especially at the logical level.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:31.780
What I want to do next is talk about the physical architectures of distributed systems, but

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:36.219
before that, since we mentioned a little bit of middleware, it's also worth mentioning

00:35:36.219 --> 00:35:41.219
some of the challenges that can appear when designing middleware and then what other,

00:35:41.219 --> 00:35:48.039
say, functionality that middleware has, especially when it comes to, again, defining, let's say,

00:35:48.039 --> 00:35:50.279
architectures in terms of components.

00:35:52.279 --> 00:35:54.879
So we already mentioned the middleware, right?

00:35:54.879 --> 00:36:00.079
It's this layer that provides a uniform interface for all of the applications that are running

00:36:00.079 --> 00:36:02.719
on top of this over-distributing system.

00:36:02.719 --> 00:36:07.819
The layer itself can run across multiple computers that can have different operating systems and

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:11.760
library versions and CPU architectures in the middle,

00:36:11.860 --> 00:36:15.640
where we'll provide a common language between all of them.

00:36:15.740 --> 00:36:18.559
It will allow applications to either communicate with other applications

00:36:18.559 --> 00:36:20.039
using that common interface,

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.880
or will allow a larger application to run across multiple computers as well.

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:32.400
So the distributed system typically contains commonly used components

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:36.200
and functions that need not be implemented by applications separately.

00:36:36.199 --> 00:36:44.079
So inside the database, we include a set of common functions and components that all of the applications work on.

00:36:45.779 --> 00:36:51.819
Now, one of the main, or one of the challenges that appears when designing middleware,

00:36:51.919 --> 00:37:01.500
and when implementing distributed systems, is the problem of having to integrate an interface with legacy applications.

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:14.199
So, as we mentioned before, a distributed system is a system that will grow in size in order to satisfy some sort of requirement, a number of users or a number of requests, right?

00:37:14.199 --> 00:37:21.440
And then we have decentralized systems that increase in size in order to integrate other systems together, right?

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:27.679
So especially in the decentralized, let's say, systems world, there's going to be the

00:37:27.679 --> 00:37:32.079
chance that you would encounter that you would need to integrate your system with another

00:37:32.079 --> 00:37:36.460
existing system, but that uses different kinds of libraries, maybe older version of

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:41.760
those libraries, uses older version of programming languages, and you have to now interface with

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:42.360
those languages.

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:48.019
So in order to be able to interface with them, since you've designed already a distributed

00:37:48.019 --> 00:37:53.380
and you have a set of functions and a set of components that speak a certain language,

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:55.159
means that if you want to interact with the other components,

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:57.900
you have to figure out a way to bridge that gap.

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:05.139
So the solution inside distributed systems is to implement wrappers or adapters for legacy components.

00:38:05.139 --> 00:38:08.340
So for components that do not offer an appropriate interface,

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:12.880
what we do is we create a wrapper or adapter interface on top of that component.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:23.740
Now, when creating, let's say, wrappers, there are, of course, a few challenges that can appear depending on what strategy you use to implement the wrappers.

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:31.220
So let's say that we have a distributive system with three components and an application that needs to interact with the distributive system.

00:38:31.960 --> 00:38:37.980
If the application wants to interact with the distributive system, the application needs to be able to interact with each of the three components.

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.880
The components also have to be able to interact with the application.

00:38:41.860 --> 00:38:48.340
This means that if somehow the components or the application contain, let's say, legacy interfaces,

00:38:48.559 --> 00:38:52.980
it means that we would technically need to build some sort of wrapper or adapter

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:56.720
between all the components and all the applications involved.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:04.760
So this would require an n-square number of wrappers and adapters to be implemented inside your distributor system.

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:06.340
So this would be the diagram on the net.

00:39:06.980 --> 00:39:09.600
So this would be very hard to maintain, right?

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:12.500
To implement so many adapters and interfaces

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:14.740
that have to be maintained by programmers

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:16.360
will be very expensive.

00:39:16.519 --> 00:39:18.019
I think even if you were to use AI,

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:19.240
you will burn through a lot of tokens

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:20.380
to be able to maintain them.

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:22.820
So it might not be really the best of solutions there.

00:39:24.579 --> 00:39:27.200
Now, another solution would be to use a broker.

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:29.880
And this is a very typical solution in distributed systems.

00:39:30.039 --> 00:39:31.120
So inside the middleware,

00:39:31.519 --> 00:39:33.940
you implement a service that acts as a broker

00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:35.660
between these legacy components.

00:39:35.659 --> 00:39:47.219
So the legacy component, instead of communicating directly with the applications, with the legacy applications or components, they will actually communicate with the broker, and then the broker will implement an adapter or wrapper.

00:39:48.759 --> 00:39:59.940
So this now allows you to reduce the number of wrappers and adapters that you need to implement to 2N, where N is the number of components, which makes it be more tractable.

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:12.960
Of course, you're adding some performance overhead, because now instead of having the applications be able to communicate directly with other applications, you have to use a broker, another system that sits in the middle, or a hub.

00:40:14.059 --> 00:40:18.440
And then, of course, the broker could also pose challenges in terms of floor tolerance, right?

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:21.500
Because if the broker dies and the components, they cannot communicate with each other.

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:30.079
But of course, you can organize this broker system to be centralized at the logical level, but distributed at the physical level.

00:40:30.159 --> 00:40:35.500
So you can have multiple machines actually implement the broker, which should only be the problem of fault tolerance.

00:40:37.539 --> 00:40:44.380
So I want to make a small parenthesis because I know I mentioned, you know, you can use AI to maintain the code and everything, you know.

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:55.720
So I think, again, a lot of people, you know, talk about these days about, you know, AI and maybe replacing, you know, software engineers and, you know, let AI write the code for us.

00:40:55.780 --> 00:41:02.039
And I just want to mention again that writing the code was never really the problem, I think, in software development.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:06.140
The main problem has always been maintaining the code.

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:07.720
So how do you fix it?

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:10.420
How do you make the code work well in a specific context?

00:41:11.019 --> 00:41:12.980
And then when it comes to distributed systems,

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:16.360
actually implementing these things has never really been the challenge.

00:41:16.360 --> 00:41:20.500
The challenge has been how do we design a coherent system that works well.

00:41:21.099 --> 00:41:22.579
So that's very difficult.

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:24.760
It depends on the context a lot.

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:27.260
So it depends on what application you need to implement.

00:41:28.039 --> 00:41:30.940
With an AI, you need to give it a lot of information

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:34.099
to be able to replicate that kind of context

00:41:34.099 --> 00:41:37.360
that a seasoned, let's say, software engineer would know already.

00:41:38.079 --> 00:41:41.000
So this is where, even though these AI tools,

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:43.000
I think, again, the cat is out of the box,

00:41:43.079 --> 00:41:43.700
so they're here.

00:41:43.820 --> 00:41:45.260
We'll have to learn how to use them.

00:41:45.519 --> 00:41:47.780
I don't think we know how to use them really well right now

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:49.640
because it's a very brand new technology.

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:51.160
If you think about it,

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.099
when the computers were introduced for the first time,

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:55.519
it took many years before people figured out

00:41:55.519 --> 00:41:56.380
what to do with them.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:58.300
I think it's kind of similar with AI.

00:41:58.440 --> 00:41:59.960
Maybe we'll figure it out a little bit faster,

00:42:00.099 --> 00:42:01.460
but right now we don't really know

00:42:01.460 --> 00:42:04.880
the capabilities of these AI models.

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:08.780
We don't necessarily know exactly what they can do and what they cannot do.

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:11.940
So we're kind of learning how to operate with them.

00:42:12.039 --> 00:42:19.640
But what's very important is that for you as a software engineer, what the AI cannot replace right now is the judgment part.

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:27.980
So you can judge by looking at an application, okay, am I going to implement these wrappers and adapters using an all-to-all implementation?

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:33.539
Then, of course, the AI will be happy to generate all of the code possible to make this work.

00:42:33.539 --> 00:42:35.920
but it will become very hard to maintain, right,

00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:38.259
and very expensive to maintain because for each interface,

00:42:38.380 --> 00:42:41.639
you will burn through tokens, for example, versus using a broker.

00:42:42.119 --> 00:42:43.639
And then even if you use a broker, you know,

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:47.059
depending on the situation, do you want to make the broker distributed?

00:42:47.659 --> 00:42:49.340
Do you want to keep it as a single service?

00:42:49.719 --> 00:42:52.940
Do you want to use some sort of mechanism where you select a new leader,

00:42:53.059 --> 00:42:55.679
you know, that becomes a broker, for example, in your system?

00:42:55.779 --> 00:42:57.239
There are a lot of solutions there.

00:42:57.519 --> 00:42:59.880
But which one works better, it's very hard to determine

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:02.820
because you need a lot of information regarding the context.

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:16.120
So I'm hoping that in this class, by working on the assignments, you would be developing a little bit of judgment in terms of how you choose certain things inside your, let's say, application, inside the development of the application.

00:43:16.580 --> 00:43:16.780
Okay?

00:43:17.340 --> 00:43:22.840
So anyway, this is an example of, again, things that can look different in terms of architecture.

00:43:23.140 --> 00:43:26.840
They can solve the same problem, but one might be better than the other depending on the situation.

00:43:28.519 --> 00:43:29.780
Now, all right.

00:43:30.100 --> 00:43:31.680
I'll close a parenthesis here.

00:43:31.679 --> 00:43:32.399
now

00:43:32.399 --> 00:43:36.039
what we assume

00:43:36.039 --> 00:43:37.739
so far when we talk about these wrappers

00:43:37.739 --> 00:43:39.899
and adapters is that we have access

00:43:39.899 --> 00:43:41.440
to the code

00:43:41.440 --> 00:43:43.639
of the components, especially

00:43:43.639 --> 00:43:45.859
the legacy components, so we can modify

00:43:45.859 --> 00:43:47.500
them so that they use these wrappers

00:43:47.500 --> 00:43:49.379
and adapters between our system

00:43:49.379 --> 00:43:51.639
and then we have access to our system

00:43:51.639 --> 00:43:53.819
and we can implement those wrappers and adapters

00:43:53.819 --> 00:43:55.099
on our side as well

00:43:55.099 --> 00:43:57.599
so these wrappers and adapters have to be

00:43:57.599 --> 00:43:59.379
implemented on both sides because maybe

00:43:59.379 --> 00:44:01.519
the component wants to communicate with

00:44:01.519 --> 00:44:05.340
our components inside a distributed system and our distributed system wants to communicate with

00:44:05.340 --> 00:44:11.199
those legacy components as well. But a lot of the times we actually will not have access to the code

00:44:11.199 --> 00:44:16.440
of the legacy component. So the legacy component might actually be a compiled program, right,

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:21.400
which means that we have access to the binary code. We can, you know, disassemble the code,

00:44:21.539 --> 00:44:25.960
but this will not give us the actual code that was written in whatever programming languages was

00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:28.000
written, right, which is get the assembly

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:29.599
instructions. So

00:44:29.599 --> 00:44:32.039
sometimes it might be very hard to actually

00:44:32.039 --> 00:44:33.960
modify that legacy

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:35.960
component in order to support these wrappers

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:38.059
and adapters. So then, to be able

00:44:38.059 --> 00:44:39.380
to solve this problem

00:44:39.380 --> 00:44:41.960
of developing, let's say,

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:43.380
adaptable middleware,

00:44:43.980 --> 00:44:46.000
one of the solutions and one of the things that

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:48.000
the middleware can do is actually it can

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:49.820
intercept the

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:51.940
flow of control of a

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:53.019
specific application.

00:44:54.039 --> 00:44:55.820
It can intercept the instructions

00:44:55.820 --> 00:44:58.720
that are being executed, and it can transform them

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:01.260
into something else that is more appropriate

00:45:01.260 --> 00:45:03.840
to whatever interface we have in the distributed system.

00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:06.820
So this happens not just in distributed systems,

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:08.920
but it happens in operating systems as well.

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:11.760
I think as an example, I can give you the example

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:14.760
of what Apple and Microsoft have to do

00:45:14.760 --> 00:45:18.800
when they decided that they wanted to support applications

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:20.700
and they built an operating system

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:22.660
that runs on the ARM architecture.

00:45:23.219 --> 00:45:27.719
So one very important difference between the Apple, let's say,

00:45:28.239 --> 00:45:32.039
silicon processors and the processors that we used to use

00:45:32.039 --> 00:45:34.639
in the laptops and desktop computers

00:45:34.639 --> 00:45:36.759
is that they use a completely different architecture.

00:45:37.259 --> 00:45:39.659
So PC computers, they use the x86 architecture.

00:45:40.719 --> 00:45:43.139
In the Internet, AMD, they produce processors

00:45:43.139 --> 00:45:45.460
that follow the x86 architecture.

00:45:46.059 --> 00:45:50.179
But Apple decided to move to ARM 64 for various reasons.

00:45:50.179 --> 00:45:52.440
They had more control over their processors.

00:45:52.659 --> 00:45:57.299
They could do an on-chip design, which then allowed them to make it more efficient, and so on and so forth.

00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:02.359
But the most important aspect was that, since they had a completely different architecture,

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:05.779
they needed a completely different operating system, which they did.

00:46:05.779 --> 00:46:10.759
They implemented, they re-implemented, they ported their operating system to the ARM64 architecture,

00:46:11.379 --> 00:46:13.579
and they ported their applications as well.

00:46:13.699 --> 00:46:18.960
But all of the applications that were not built by Apple, they had to be ported as well.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:20.720
or if they were not ported, right,

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:23.500
Apple still wanted to give the users the ability to run them.

00:46:24.039 --> 00:46:26.280
So what they did is they created this layer,

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:28.079
you can call it kind of like a middleware,

00:46:28.559 --> 00:46:32.059
called Rosetta that can intercept x86 instructions

00:46:32.059 --> 00:46:35.780
and convert them dynamically into ARM64 instructions.

00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:39.300
Now, of course, it's not a very efficient way to do it, right,

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:41.039
but it allows you now to run applications

00:46:41.039 --> 00:46:42.820
that were designed for one interface

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:44.460
on a completely different interface.

00:46:44.960 --> 00:46:46.440
And Microsoft did the same thing.

00:46:46.539 --> 00:46:48.199
I think their system, maybe it's called Prism,

00:46:48.199 --> 00:46:51.779
but I don't remember exactly. But they have a similar system when you're running

00:46:51.779 --> 00:46:55.460
x86 applications on Windows 11 for ARM, for example.

00:46:56.219 --> 00:46:59.659
They also have a translation layer as well. So again,

00:46:59.879 --> 00:47:04.000
the way this works is that you have some sort of hook inside your system

00:47:04.000 --> 00:47:07.859
or inside the middleware that can capture different kind of calls or different kind of

00:47:07.859 --> 00:47:11.179
instructions and then transform them dynamically into the

00:47:11.179 --> 00:47:12.899
interface.

00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:20.579
Okay. Any questions so far regarding logical architectures in the middleware?

00:47:20.579 --> 00:47:26.400
I have a quick question just about why they made ARM64 you said?

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:27.400
Yes, yeah.

00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:33.059
Was it because they were worried about patent law or something or it was just purely they wanted a different design?

00:47:33.059 --> 00:47:39.940
They just wanted a different design so Apple has always been about having an integrated design.

00:47:39.940 --> 00:47:43.220
So integrated means that they would like to tune the hardware.

00:47:43.519 --> 00:47:45.380
They would like to tune the operating system.

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:48.920
They would like to tune the applications so that they all work together.

00:47:49.099 --> 00:47:51.400
So the Apple ecosystem is actually very nice.

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:52.639
You can have a phone.

00:47:52.780 --> 00:47:57.079
You can have a laptop and a desktop all from Apple, and they're all interconnected with each other.

00:47:57.280 --> 00:47:58.720
So you can move tabs.

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:01.039
You can move applications from one from another.

00:48:01.260 --> 00:48:02.720
You know, a tablet as well.

00:48:02.780 --> 00:48:04.579
You know, you can do all these fancy things.

00:48:05.259 --> 00:48:09.639
And I think Apple really wanted to build, again, computers.

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:11.000
Well, there were two reasons there.

00:48:11.279 --> 00:48:13.779
They wanted to build computers that were power efficient,

00:48:13.779 --> 00:48:17.840
so a laptop that can actually last for 24 hours, right?

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:22.739
The PC world, they said, oh, you cannot build that because of power constraints and whatever, you know.

00:48:23.039 --> 00:48:24.199
They put all of these reasons.

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:26.579
So Apple said, okay, we'll design a home processor.

00:48:26.579 --> 00:48:34.880
what they did is they've integrated the processor with the memory and with the system bus.

00:48:35.219 --> 00:48:39.519
So that's why on an Apple computer, everything is soldered directly to the motherboard.

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:44.039
So you cannot upgrade anymore, your processor, your RAM, everything is soldered.

00:48:44.199 --> 00:48:46.860
But because it's soldered, they can control the signals.

00:48:47.639 --> 00:48:53.840
Because they soldered the RAM, they were able to use low power DDR RAM instead of the regular RAM.

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:56.940
So they were able to, you know, save a lot of energy.

00:48:57.740 --> 00:49:03.840
It's not necessarily that the ARM64 architecture has an inherent advantage over the x86.

00:49:04.400 --> 00:49:06.340
And the differences are not really that big.

00:49:06.700 --> 00:49:10.200
But it has more to do with the fact that the ARM architecture is a clean architecture.

00:49:10.760 --> 00:49:14.539
Apple, they now have to provide backwards compatibility inside their architecture.

00:49:15.140 --> 00:49:17.280
You know, x86 or other architectures.

00:49:17.340 --> 00:49:21.460
And then they were able to optimize the communication paths on the chip.

00:49:22.019 --> 00:49:23.440
So that things are very efficient.

00:49:23.440 --> 00:49:30.780
They use, you know, low energy, you know, or they have a very good performance per watt, let's say, performance.

00:49:31.420 --> 00:49:34.260
So that's one of the reasons.

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:37.240
They wanted to build something that, again, lasted for 24 hours.

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:41.659
But then again, they also built the Mac Studios, right, which then they don't have a battery.

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:42.400
Right.

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:49.500
So then the other reason why they built this system is because they wanted to build systems that, I think,

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:53.800
that were suited for the AI workloads that they anticipated.

00:49:54.119 --> 00:49:57.239
I think Apple, you know, had some breath in the mind there.

00:49:57.300 --> 00:50:02.260
I think they anticipated that, okay, so LLMs are becoming a thing,

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:03.800
AI is becoming a thing.

00:50:03.940 --> 00:50:06.940
I think a lot of people will want to run local models on their computers.

00:50:07.460 --> 00:50:09.840
So what would be the best way for us to build computers

00:50:09.840 --> 00:50:12.159
that have a lot of RAM that is also very fast,

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:14.679
that people would buy to run models on?

00:50:14.679 --> 00:50:29.319
So that's where they, I wouldn't say that they innovated because those architectures existed in supercomputers as well, but they adopted and they chose their architectures in order to satisfy this AI workload.

00:50:29.940 --> 00:50:36.460
So what's also very unique about Apple is how they view the unified memories,

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:38.660
the unified between processor and GPU.

00:50:38.880 --> 00:50:44.940
So in Apple, the processor and the GPU can actually access the exact same physical memory,

00:50:45.300 --> 00:50:50.620
of course, at different speeds, but without any translation between applications.

00:50:51.140 --> 00:50:57.940
Now, the way, for example, a process in a PC computer or a laptop

00:50:57.940 --> 00:50:59.980
accesses the memory of a GPU

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:02.179
is you have to actually create a clone

00:51:02.179 --> 00:51:03.840
of the memory. So

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:06.019
the memory of the GPU, right, the GPU

00:51:06.019 --> 00:51:07.340
has its own dedicated memory,

00:51:07.780 --> 00:51:09.820
but it has to be mapped to the physical

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:12.039
memory. So you're storing stuff

00:51:12.039 --> 00:51:13.840
in the physical memory and then

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:15.000
using GPU

00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:17.920
instructions to transfer memory from the

00:51:17.920 --> 00:51:19.820
memory to the GPU to then

00:51:19.820 --> 00:51:21.200
do the operations on the GPU.

00:51:21.700 --> 00:51:23.679
On the Apple computer, because they had

00:51:23.679 --> 00:51:25.820
control over the architecture, they were able

00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:27.820
to design a chip where the GPU can

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:33.340
access the main memory directly no translation needed no transfers needed this is their innovation

00:51:33.340 --> 00:51:39.500
which was actually done very well and from that point of view the apple computers are superior

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:46.180
especially for ai workloads anyways amd and intel tried to replicate that nvidia tried to replicate

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:52.740
that right by creating unified architectures but they're not really unified so with the

00:51:52.739 --> 00:51:59.019
AMD Ryzen AI Max Plus whatever the name is right for the long name of chip that they have.

00:51:59.099 --> 00:52:03.659
They also sell with 120 gigabytes of RAM that can also be used for the GPU.

00:52:03.839 --> 00:52:06.599
Keep in mind that there's still some copying that happens.

00:52:06.859 --> 00:52:09.399
It's not a pure unified memory architecture.

00:52:09.399 --> 00:52:16.479
And then for NVIDIA's DGX SparkBox that also doesn't have a pure unified memory

00:52:16.479 --> 00:52:21.819
because the GPU has a bunch of memory and then the rest of the ARM processor

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:24.500
so it has a bunch of memory, and they operate at different speeds.

00:52:25.700 --> 00:52:28.140
So you have to be aware on how you transfer the data.

00:52:28.580 --> 00:52:31.740
The CUDA library can transfer the data for you, can try to detect,

00:52:31.820 --> 00:52:34.000
but you can also do manual transfer of data as well

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:36.000
to make your application be a bit more efficient.

00:52:36.180 --> 00:52:39.340
So anyways, in the end, that was the reason why they switched to this ARM

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:40.760
so that they can have full control.

00:52:40.860 --> 00:52:44.480
But I think they predicted that that's the kind of stuff that people would use,

00:52:44.760 --> 00:52:48.019
so they wanted to be the first ones in the market that had computers for that,

00:52:48.019 --> 00:52:52.820
So, which, I think in the end, they ended up being correct.

00:52:54.519 --> 00:52:55.820
All right, any other questions?

00:52:59.519 --> 00:53:00.019
All right.

00:53:00.519 --> 00:53:05.920
Well, if there are no other questions, then let's start talking about how do we organize

00:53:08.019 --> 00:53:10.119
distributed systems from a physical point of view.

00:53:10.119 --> 00:53:12.519
We'll talk about the physical architectures.

00:53:12.519 --> 00:53:17.019
And before we talk actual distributed architectures, I think it's very important to

00:53:17.019 --> 00:53:22.360
understand centralized system architectures and more specifically the

00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:28.179
client server model. So this is a very popular model I kind of want to say that

00:53:28.179 --> 00:53:32.420
a great very great majority of applications that exist in the cloud in

00:53:32.420 --> 00:53:37.900
the web or phones you know they follow this client server model it's actually

00:53:37.900 --> 00:53:42.599
very simple model to understand and to use and then from this model we actually

00:53:42.599 --> 00:53:46.799
we can derive more complex distributor systems models.

00:53:47.699 --> 00:53:50.539
So the client-server model has a few characteristics.

00:53:50.960 --> 00:53:52.440
It's, again, very easy to understand.

00:53:52.579 --> 00:53:58.339
What we do is we organize a client-server system into processes.

00:53:58.559 --> 00:54:01.819
The processes can have one of two roles.

00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:06.579
The processes can be clients or they can act as servers.

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:09.639
The servers, they offer services.

00:54:09.639 --> 00:54:13.039
So they have computational functions, resources, right?

00:54:13.500 --> 00:54:14.699
That the clients can access.

00:54:14.839 --> 00:54:18.379
So the clients, they usually make use of those services provided by the servers.

00:54:19.299 --> 00:54:22.599
The clients and the servers can be deployed on different machines.

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:25.699
They can be co-located, but they can also run on different machines.

00:54:27.099 --> 00:54:31.219
And then the most important property is that the clients,

00:54:31.299 --> 00:54:34.920
they follow a request-reply model regarding using the services.

00:54:35.539 --> 00:54:41.619
So whenever a client wants to use a service from a server, it needs to submit a request.

00:54:42.539 --> 00:54:45.320
It sends that request to some sort of communication mechanism.

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:46.820
The server receives it.

00:54:47.139 --> 00:54:53.320
The server then processes the response and then sends back the response using the same communication mechanism.

00:54:53.320 --> 00:55:00.720
So here in this diagram, you can see that we have a process which represents the client and a process which represents the server.

00:55:00.719 --> 00:55:05.239
If the client wants to make use of a service provided by the server, he sends a request,

00:55:05.779 --> 00:55:08.659
he then waits for the server to process the reply,

00:55:09.259 --> 00:55:12.819
and then the server will send back the response or the reply to the client.

00:55:13.339 --> 00:55:17.599
Okay, this is the most easy way to understand client-server model.

00:55:17.799 --> 00:55:19.059
Again, it exists everywhere.

00:55:19.219 --> 00:55:24.879
When you're using your application, when you're using Gmail or Outlook to access your email,

00:55:25.419 --> 00:55:28.419
basically your Outlook application acts as a client,

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:31.260
and then it connects to a server in Microsoft to receive the email.

00:55:31.659 --> 00:55:32.280
Very simple.

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:35.800
When you go to YouTube and you request a video, client-server model.

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:37.639
You're sending a request, you're receiving the email.

00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:39.519
Okay, very good.

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:45.240
Now, let's see how do we integrate these logical architectures

00:55:45.240 --> 00:55:51.280
that we talked about last week with this physical way of organizing

00:55:51.280 --> 00:55:54.980
a centralized system or a distributed system.

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:59.639
So let's start with the application layering architecture.

00:55:59.920 --> 00:56:06.619
So if you remember, application layering, it's a layered architecture where you split your application to three layers.

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:13.199
One layer is responsible with managing the user interface, another layer for processing, and another layer for the data.

00:56:13.199 --> 00:56:16.159
now of course

00:56:16.159 --> 00:56:18.539
you can for example

00:56:18.539 --> 00:56:20.819
still implement

00:56:20.819 --> 00:56:22.619
a let's say client server

00:56:22.619 --> 00:56:24.699
application where

00:56:24.699 --> 00:56:26.399
the entire user interface

00:56:26.399 --> 00:56:28.879
and the entire processing layer and the entire

00:56:28.879 --> 00:56:30.559
data layer resides on the server

00:56:30.559 --> 00:56:33.079
so then the client

00:56:33.079 --> 00:56:35.059
basically connects to the server

00:56:35.059 --> 00:56:36.819
through some sort of other protocol and then

00:56:36.819 --> 00:56:38.559
runs the application on the server

00:56:38.559 --> 00:56:41.000
so this is called a single tiered

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:42.559
architecture where

00:56:42.559 --> 00:56:45.320
basically the client

00:56:45.320 --> 00:56:47.480
connects to some sort of terminal interface

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:49.259
to the server. So let's say

00:56:49.259 --> 00:56:51.340
for example that I want to

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:53.219
ask you after you implement programming

00:56:53.219 --> 00:56:55.099
assignment 2 and you build the search engine

00:56:55.099 --> 00:56:57.239
I would

00:56:57.239 --> 00:56:59.179
tell you that hey I want to run

00:56:59.179 --> 00:57:01.000
this search engine on

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:03.279
Chameleon Cloud but then I'd like for you

00:57:03.279 --> 00:57:04.940
to be able to connect to that server

00:57:04.940 --> 00:57:07.219
you know file retrieval engine

00:57:07.219 --> 00:57:09.139
from Chameleon Cloud I'd like you to be able to connect

00:57:09.139 --> 00:57:11.079
from your laptop and your desktop computer

00:57:11.079 --> 00:57:15.259
let's see. The easiest way that you would connect and run the application

00:57:15.259 --> 00:57:18.759
is by SSH-ing into that machine and running the application

00:57:18.759 --> 00:57:22.480
directly from that machine. This is an example of a single-tiered architecture.

00:57:23.239 --> 00:57:27.059
There's nothing that happens that gets processed on the client side. You just connect

00:57:27.059 --> 00:57:31.159
to a terminal to the server and you're accessing the resources on the server or you're accessing

00:57:31.159 --> 00:57:35.420
the application. Now, of course, we can also

00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:43.240
create a two-tier

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:46.840
literature, where now we split part of the application

00:57:46.840 --> 00:57:50.700
and we'll put one part of the application on the client and one part of the application

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:54.780
on the server. Now keep in mind that the application is organized in layers.

00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:58.800
So the question is, how do we split these layers between the client

00:57:58.800 --> 00:58:02.460
machine and the server machine? The diagrams that we have here on the bottom

00:58:02.460 --> 00:58:05.519
represents the various ways you can split those layers.

00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:09.179
So in diagram A, you can split, for example,

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:11.920
part of the user interface can be executed on the client.

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:15.980
The remaining user interface, application layer and data layer

00:58:15.980 --> 00:58:18.000
can be implemented on the server.

00:58:18.840 --> 00:58:22.960
You can have the entire user interface running on the client in diagram B,

00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:27.300
and then the processing layer and the data layer be executed on the server side.

00:58:27.920 --> 00:58:29.599
You can split the application layer.

00:58:29.800 --> 00:58:31.579
You can split the data layer as well.

00:58:31.579 --> 00:58:38.719
Okay, so let's give some examples of all of these ways of configuring a two-tiered architecture.

00:58:39.099 --> 00:58:44.079
So whenever you hear web applications, right, so websites,

00:58:44.840 --> 00:58:47.739
they can be implemented or they're usually implemented

00:58:47.739 --> 00:58:52.639
by either following configuration from diagram B or from diagram C.

00:58:52.940 --> 00:58:54.920
I kind of want to say that 26 years ago,

00:58:55.559 --> 00:59:00.279
websites were implemented following the configurations from diagram B.

00:59:01.040 --> 00:59:04.460
Basically, the user interface was computed on the client.

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:06.980
So this would be your web browser.

00:59:07.260 --> 00:59:12.920
Your web browser will receive the HTML page and will render the HTML page on your computer.

00:59:13.060 --> 00:59:17.360
So the user interface part was solely done on the client machine.

00:59:17.820 --> 00:59:22.640
And then in the server, you have the backend that took care of the logic of your website,

00:59:22.640 --> 00:59:26.160
and the database that stored your data related to your website.

00:59:26.960 --> 00:59:32.699
Nowadays, modern-day web applications actually fall into the category of diagram C

00:59:32.699 --> 00:59:38.519
because a part of the application from the server gets actually sent to the browser

00:59:38.519 --> 00:59:40.679
under the form of JavaScript code.

00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:43.399
They can actually run code in your browser

00:59:43.399 --> 00:59:47.379
and do certain kinds of operations that are part of the processing layer.

00:59:47.659 --> 00:59:50.839
And then the server will still do its own processing,

00:59:51.199 --> 00:59:53.099
and the data resides always on the servers.

00:59:53.099 --> 00:59:57.719
Now, let's see if we can look at other configurations.

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:00.039
So, let's see at configuration A.

01:00:00.480 --> 01:00:07.679
I think the best example that I can give you is the attempt that Google tried to make

01:00:07.679 --> 01:00:10.759
to build a gaming-as-a-service platform.

01:00:11.380 --> 01:00:15.940
So, again, if you play video games, you might have heard that Google was trying to build

01:00:15.940 --> 01:00:19.259
a cloud that allowed you to actually play a game in the cloud.

01:00:19.699 --> 01:00:22.360
So, for example, you could play it from any computer, right?

01:00:22.360 --> 01:00:26.780
If your computer didn't have a lot of computational power to render the game,

01:00:26.880 --> 01:00:29.160
rendering would be done on the server,

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:31.220
the logic of the game would be done on the server,

01:00:31.460 --> 01:00:33.099
and then the game itself, the files of the game,

01:00:33.140 --> 01:00:34.099
would be stored on the server.

01:00:34.640 --> 01:00:37.220
So if you look at Configuration A,

01:00:37.780 --> 01:00:41.099
this is how we would describe that two-tiered architecture

01:00:41.099 --> 01:00:42.340
that Google tried to build.

01:00:42.840 --> 01:00:46.640
Part of the user interface would be processed on your machine.

01:00:47.420 --> 01:00:49.720
The rest of the user interface, which is the rendering,

01:00:49.720 --> 01:00:52.320
probably, of the beta game, will be processed on the server,

01:00:52.360 --> 01:00:58.360
The logic of the game is on the cloud, you know, in Google, and then the data for the game is also in the cloud as well.

01:00:58.380 --> 01:01:03.160
Can Google Drive be an example of a Google Drive?

01:01:03.539 --> 01:01:13.579
Yeah, so Google Drive, let's see, so Google Drive, it's a bit more simplistic because Google Drive itself doesn't really have a very fancy interface.

01:01:13.579 --> 01:01:19.140
We're typically accessing it using a web browser, right, and it just manages the data.

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:21.740
So the processing layer is not very sophisticated.

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:24.480
You cannot really do sophisticated processing

01:01:24.480 --> 01:01:25.519
inside Google Drive.

01:01:25.820 --> 01:01:29.519
So I would say it still falls in the area of B or C.

01:01:30.360 --> 01:01:32.519
Mostly B because, again, the application,

01:01:32.880 --> 01:01:35.039
Google Drive doesn't really run JavaScript

01:01:35.039 --> 01:01:37.360
on your browser, I think.

01:01:37.480 --> 01:01:39.920
I think they do some operations to cache data,

01:01:40.400 --> 01:01:43.039
but not really to process data.

01:01:44.079 --> 01:01:46.300
It could also fall in the category of B

01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:47.740
if you eliminate the application,

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:50.980
where maybe part of the data gets cached on your browser,

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:53.780
but then the rest of the data resides in your cloud.

01:01:54.539 --> 01:02:01.639
So, for example, for configuration E,

01:02:01.740 --> 01:02:04.940
another example that is similar is when you do software development.

01:02:05.579 --> 01:02:08.159
So, let's say you do software development on your local app.

01:02:08.860 --> 01:02:13.780
The way this would work is you would open some sort of code editor or IDE.

01:02:14.199 --> 01:02:15.460
It has a user interface.

01:02:15.760 --> 01:02:20.940
It has a processing layer where you have the compilers and analyzers for code.

01:02:21.400 --> 01:02:25.400
And what you do is you do a git clone to clone the data on your computer.

01:02:25.800 --> 01:02:28.960
So store a copy of the data on your computer, you process it,

01:02:29.199 --> 01:02:31.639
and then you resynchronize your data with the data on the server.

01:02:31.800 --> 01:02:34.940
So GitHub stores only your code,

01:02:35.039 --> 01:02:37.880
but doesn't really run any part of your ID

01:02:37.880 --> 01:02:39.219
or any part of the process.

01:02:39.219 --> 01:02:42.340
It could run nowadays with continuous integration,

01:02:42.619 --> 01:02:43.840
continuous development systems,

01:02:44.000 --> 01:02:46.119
but let's say the old school way, it would not.

01:02:46.679 --> 01:02:49.360
So that's where you have the data,

01:02:49.360 --> 01:02:53.559
is now split between the client and the server, they both store different parts.

01:02:53.559 --> 01:02:59.680
And then you can also have applications where the user interface and the processing all

01:02:59.680 --> 01:03:04.440
happens on the client side and then the data resides on the database side.

01:03:04.440 --> 01:03:09.500
So let's say, for example, if you want to access a database on a different machine,

01:03:09.500 --> 01:03:14.140
an SQL database, for example, if it's a Postgres database, you can use pgadmin.

01:03:14.139 --> 01:03:17.139
So, pgAdmin is an application, right?

01:03:17.139 --> 01:03:18.319
That you can run locally.

01:03:18.319 --> 01:03:19.539
It's a client application.

01:03:19.539 --> 01:03:22.139
So, it runs the UI there,

01:03:22.139 --> 01:03:25.440
and you can do some partial processing inside the UI.

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:26.739
Well, it actually kind of falls more

01:03:26.739 --> 01:03:29.879
into the category of a diagram C

01:03:29.879 --> 01:03:31.719
because you can do partial processing

01:03:31.719 --> 01:03:34.379
on the client as well.

01:03:37.879 --> 01:03:38.739
Where you think?

01:03:38.739 --> 01:03:39.900
Does the system be deep

01:03:39.900 --> 01:03:42.119
where it's like you have the game

01:03:42.119 --> 01:03:46.960
And then what you did in the game is from the database and it updates.

01:03:48.500 --> 01:03:52.460
Yeah, you can do a multiplayer game where that runs on the server,

01:03:52.599 --> 01:03:55.259
where the data is on the server, but then the processing layer

01:03:55.259 --> 01:03:58.759
and the user interface gets processed on your machine.

01:03:58.880 --> 01:03:59.539
Yeah, that's true.

01:03:59.659 --> 01:04:01.339
So that's actually a very good example.

01:04:01.739 --> 01:04:03.339
Yeah, a very good example.

01:04:03.440 --> 01:04:07.359
I didn't think about it, but yeah, that's a very good example of an application

01:04:07.359 --> 01:04:08.940
because the data resides on the server.

01:04:09.420 --> 01:04:11.440
The server sometimes might do some processing there,

01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:16.019
so you'll be aware it might be, you know, Diagram C as well but you know,

01:04:16.019 --> 01:04:20.880
you could have the data stored directly on the server and then all the logic

01:04:20.880 --> 01:04:25.920
it's completely on the product. Okay, all right, very good. Now of course you can

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:29.920
split the application layer in a three-tier architecture as well where you

01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:33.380
put in either each layer or subsets of the layers on three different machines

01:04:33.380 --> 01:04:38.119
and you can go, of course you can create an n-tiered architecture if you wanted to.

01:04:38.119 --> 01:04:51.599
So this way of, you know, let's say, organizing a layered architecture using the client-server model can be applied to, of course, other layered architectures, not just the application layering architecture.

01:04:52.599 --> 01:05:06.319
But the most important thing that we have to remember is that if you have a layered architecture, where, again, in order for layer one to do its operation is to call functions from layer two, which is to, you know, use services from layer three and so on and so forth.

01:05:06.320 --> 01:05:09.300
just keep in mind that the wait time will increase.

01:05:09.880 --> 01:05:12.580
So for example, let's say that we have a client machine

01:05:12.580 --> 01:05:15.860
that wants to communicate with some sort of server here

01:05:15.860 --> 01:05:17.559
that needs to communicate with a data server.

01:05:18.700 --> 01:05:19.260
Right.

01:05:19.380 --> 01:05:21.640
So when the client sends a request,

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:24.620
the client machine acts as a client

01:05:24.620 --> 01:05:28.440
while the server acts as a server

01:05:28.440 --> 01:05:29.760
and receives the request.

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:31.980
But the server also has to act as a client

01:05:31.980 --> 01:05:35.100
and send a request to the data server,

01:05:35.099 --> 01:05:38.079
which then the data server needs to send a response

01:05:38.079 --> 01:05:43.799
and then after the processing server receives the response

01:05:43.799 --> 01:05:45.440
it needs to send the response back to the client

01:05:45.440 --> 01:05:48.719
which means that the client needs to wait for that entire wait time.

01:05:48.719 --> 01:05:53.400
So the deeper the tiered architecture is

01:05:53.400 --> 01:05:55.219
the more you'll probably need to wait.

01:05:55.559 --> 01:05:58.579
The more the client will need to wait for all those requests to go through.

01:05:58.980 --> 01:06:03.519
But as you can see it's an easy way to organize an application.

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:08.079
And by using the client-server model, you cannot deploy it across multiple machines as well.

01:06:10.739 --> 01:06:14.820
Of course, this can be applied to the service-oriented model as well,

01:06:14.820 --> 01:06:16.960
where you're sending the request for different objects.

01:06:17.139 --> 01:06:19.480
It doesn't have to be necessary only for the layered architecture.

01:06:22.239 --> 01:06:28.500
Now, let's look at some other properties of client-server models,

01:06:28.639 --> 01:06:31.360
especially when it comes to the sharing of resources.

01:06:31.360 --> 01:06:35.740
More specifically, the sharing of data, let's say, under the form of files.

01:06:37.120 --> 01:06:42.240
So, again, just knowing that you can send these requests and these responses,

01:06:42.480 --> 01:06:49.519
it's not usually enough to be able to reason about how do you organize an application, a distributed application.

01:06:50.360 --> 01:06:54.780
So, when it comes to the sharing of data, and more specifically the sharing of files,

01:06:55.360 --> 01:07:00.880
people have identified actually two kinds of access models for accessing that data.

01:07:00.880 --> 01:07:06.640
You can use an upload-download model, or you can use a remote access model for accessing the data.

01:07:07.039 --> 01:07:08.880
Now, let's see what the difference between them are.

01:07:09.019 --> 01:07:10.720
Let's see, again, you have a client and a server.

01:07:11.180 --> 01:07:14.840
Now, let's look first at the diagram on the right at the upload-download model.

01:07:14.940 --> 01:07:21.360
So, in the upload-download model, if a client machine wants to access a file from the server,

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:25.940
what the client needs to do is to send a request to the server.

01:07:26.480 --> 01:07:30.059
The server needs to transfer the file to the client.

01:07:30.059 --> 01:07:38.259
The client will then need to do operations on the file locally, and then it will need to send back that file to the server.

01:07:40.820 --> 01:07:42.539
Now, why do we need to do this?

01:07:43.019 --> 01:07:49.840
Let's take a look at the example of how we do file operations on a Unix slash Linux-based system.

01:07:49.840 --> 01:08:01.700
So does anyone remember what are the four main operations or system calls that you need to use in order to interact with files on a Linux slash Unix basis?

01:08:02.240 --> 01:08:03.600
I'm sure you've heard about them.

01:08:03.680 --> 01:08:08.539
If you know the system calls, then you can implement more advanced libraries for reading and writing data.

01:08:08.539 --> 01:08:10.240
You can do very fancy stuff.

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:13.039
But you're always going to use at least these four functions.

01:08:16.180 --> 01:08:17.400
You have to open the file?

01:08:17.680 --> 01:08:18.500
Open the file, right?

01:08:18.500 --> 01:08:22.159
So you have to open the file, you can then read and write to the file,

01:08:22.199 --> 01:08:23.399
and then you have to close the file.

01:08:23.479 --> 01:08:25.640
These are the four operations, right?

01:08:25.659 --> 01:08:28.520
So if I want to build a system, right,

01:08:28.520 --> 01:08:32.060
that allows me to access files from a server, right,

01:08:32.199 --> 01:08:35.239
and if the system is a very good distributed system,

01:08:35.399 --> 01:08:37.300
it means it has good transparency.

01:08:37.880 --> 01:08:41.140
It means that I shouldn't have to modify my program to tell it to,

01:08:41.359 --> 01:08:43.779
hey, download this file, modify it, and then download it back.

01:08:44.020 --> 01:08:46.140
It should be pretty transparent, right?

01:08:46.159 --> 01:08:48.300
So I should be able to use the exact same program

01:08:48.300 --> 01:08:52.539
and it should work. Well, systems similar to Dropbox

01:08:52.539 --> 01:08:56.039
or the File Transfer Protocol, that's what they attempt to do.

01:08:56.640 --> 01:09:00.260
So with Dropbox or OneDrive or Google Drive, when you download

01:09:00.260 --> 01:09:04.220
the client program in your computer, it tries to act as a

01:09:04.220 --> 01:09:08.380
distributed system that manages your files in the cloud and it synchronizes your

01:09:08.380 --> 01:09:12.260
files with your computer. But Dropbox uses this upload-download

01:09:12.260 --> 01:09:16.380
model. So whenever you want to edit a file or read

01:09:16.380 --> 01:09:18.619
the file from Drawbox, it needs to transfer

01:09:18.619 --> 01:09:19.739
the file from the cloud.

01:09:20.100 --> 01:09:22.380
If you read it, then that's fine. You don't have to do anything

01:09:22.380 --> 01:09:24.500
special about it. But if you modify

01:09:24.500 --> 01:09:26.400
it, you modify it locally using the

01:09:26.400 --> 01:09:28.300
regular system calls, and

01:09:28.300 --> 01:09:30.119
then the client program will automatically

01:09:30.119 --> 01:09:32.460
synchronize the file back to the cloud.

01:09:34.480 --> 01:09:36.380
Alright, now another way

01:09:36.380 --> 01:09:38.279
that you can implement this, if you want

01:09:38.279 --> 01:09:40.000
to have even better transparency

01:09:40.000 --> 01:09:42.260
and if you want to avoid certain

01:09:42.260 --> 01:09:43.739
let's say performance products,

01:09:44.199 --> 01:09:45.900
is you can implement

01:09:45.899 --> 01:09:50.079
the remote access model. So for the remote access model, I still have

01:09:50.079 --> 01:09:53.759
the situation where a client wants to access the data

01:09:53.759 --> 01:09:57.799
of a file that resides on a different server. But now instead of transferring

01:09:57.799 --> 01:10:01.079
the file from the server to the client and then back from the client to the server,

01:10:01.460 --> 01:10:04.920
what I'm going to do is I'm actually going to transfer the computation

01:10:04.920 --> 01:10:09.519
from the client to the server. So now whenever the client wants to do an

01:10:09.519 --> 01:10:13.639
open system call, a closed system call, or read or write,

01:10:13.640 --> 01:10:18.840
Those operations will now actually be forwarded from the client to the server.

01:10:19.480 --> 01:10:24.420
So if I want to modify, let's say, 100 megabytes in a 4-gigabyte file,

01:10:24.860 --> 01:10:27.660
I want to modify maybe somewhere in the middle, right?

01:10:27.720 --> 01:10:31.900
I just need to send that operation, send 100 bytes of data,

01:10:32.360 --> 01:10:35.300
and then the operation will actually happen on the server side.

01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:39.539
So we can already see one advantage of the remote access model, right,

01:10:39.539 --> 01:10:44.159
is that I don't have to transfer the entire data to do an operation on that data.

01:10:45.180 --> 01:10:49.579
The disadvantage is that I don't have any duplicates of the data on the client machine.

01:10:50.260 --> 01:10:53.539
So the client actually does not store that data in any form whatsoever.

01:10:54.279 --> 01:10:56.619
Maybe parts of the data might reside in RAM,

01:10:56.619 --> 01:10:59.199
in some sort of buffers when I do these operations.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:01.680
But whenever I close the file,

01:11:02.060 --> 01:11:04.960
the file will reside on the server, not on the client.

01:11:05.319 --> 01:11:08.760
So for a very large file, if I just need to modify 100 megabytes,

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:12.140
a 4 gigabyte file with the upload download model, I've got to transfer

01:11:12.140 --> 01:11:15.880
4 gigabytes, modify 100 megabytes, and then transfer the data back.

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:18.699
That's a lot of data that needs to be transferred back and forth.

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:22.739
With the remote access, I just need to send that operation,

01:11:22.880 --> 01:11:26.760
the right operation, to the server and perform that operation directly on the server.

01:11:27.840 --> 01:11:31.460
So there is a system that implements the remote access model.

01:11:32.500 --> 01:11:34.920
It is still very popular

01:11:34.920 --> 01:11:37.239
in units-based and Linux-based systems.

01:11:37.239 --> 01:11:42.699
It used to be the main file system for supercomputers 30 years ago.

01:11:43.199 --> 01:11:47.039
Now they use a parallel or distributed file system,

01:11:47.279 --> 01:11:52.000
which is kind of a network file system that can store the data on multiple servers simultaneously.

01:11:52.679 --> 01:11:59.199
But the network file system, or NFS, is a very popular solution for implementing, again,

01:11:59.260 --> 01:12:02.539
file sharing between a server and multiple clients.

01:12:02.539 --> 01:12:09.039
The way the network file system works is you run some programs, both on the client side

01:12:09.039 --> 01:12:13.600
and on the server side, and they transform those operations for you.

01:12:14.159 --> 01:12:20.199
We say that the client machines will mount folders or files from the server machine,

01:12:20.380 --> 01:12:25.479
so they get attached to the file system structure, and they appear to the user as if they are

01:12:25.479 --> 01:12:29.960
normal files, but whenever we access them, the operations happen on the server.

01:12:30.439 --> 01:12:33.880
Now, how does the network file system implement this?

01:12:34.220 --> 01:12:42.619
It implements it actually through the use of a layer or a middleware layer that is actually provided by the operating system.

01:12:43.220 --> 01:12:51.420
So modern-day Unix and Linux-based systems, what they do is they implement a layer called the virtual file system layer, or VFS.

01:12:51.420 --> 01:13:05.520
So this means that any single time your program does a system call to operate on files, the system call gets captured by the operating system and gets sent to the virtual file system layer library.

01:13:05.520 --> 01:13:23.400
The virtual file system layer library then makes a choice, depending if the file resides, for example, locally on your machine, it will forward that operation to the local file system interface, which will then be able to do those operations directly on the file that is stored locally.

01:13:24.219 --> 01:13:30.559
If you're doing this operation over a file or a directory that is mounted from a server,

01:13:30.960 --> 01:13:32.739
so it resides on a remote machine,

01:13:32.739 --> 01:13:40.239
then the virtual file system layer will do a call to the network file system client program.

01:13:40.579 --> 01:13:44.399
And then the client program will use some sort of communication mechanism.

01:13:44.779 --> 01:13:49.099
Let's say the original network file system implementation used a remote procedure call.

01:13:49.100 --> 01:13:56.579
So it would do a remote procedure call to a server stop, which will then forward the operation to the network file system server.

01:13:56.940 --> 01:14:04.980
And then the network file system server will forward the operation to the virtual file system layer of the server machine, which will then make that decision again.

01:14:05.260 --> 01:14:12.560
And since the file resides locally on the server machine, it will actually forward that operation to the local file system interface of the server.

01:14:13.420 --> 01:14:15.280
It means to return any data, right?

01:14:15.340 --> 01:14:19.160
When you do a read operation, you get, you know, the contents of the data that you read

01:14:19.160 --> 01:14:20.740
plus the amount of data that you read.

01:14:21.020 --> 01:14:25.400
When you write operation, you get the amount of bytes that you wrote to the file.

01:14:25.480 --> 01:14:29.280
So you get back that information to the callback of that remote procedure call

01:14:29.280 --> 01:14:31.120
of that message that was being sent.

01:14:31.420 --> 01:14:34.260
But basically, it works in a request-reply fashion.

01:14:34.720 --> 01:14:38.460
So the network fire system client sends a request to the server,

01:14:38.820 --> 01:14:41.280
he performs the approbation, and then he gives back a reply.

01:14:41.279 --> 01:14:42.939
every single time.

01:14:43.599 --> 01:14:46.539
That's how the read, write, open, close functions work.

01:14:47.019 --> 01:14:48.599
There's always some sort of return

01:14:48.599 --> 01:14:49.579
that you get back from.

01:14:51.800 --> 01:14:54.219
Okay, now one small parenthesis here.

01:14:54.579 --> 01:14:56.159
I don't know if I mentioned last time

01:14:56.159 --> 01:14:57.479
regarding drawbox.

01:14:57.559 --> 01:14:59.219
This is more of a historical take.

01:14:59.699 --> 01:15:03.119
Again, let me know if I said it or not,

01:15:03.179 --> 01:15:03.819
but basically,

01:15:04.359 --> 01:15:07.759
regarding how drawbox used to run

01:15:07.759 --> 01:15:10.779
initially when it was initially created,

01:15:10.779 --> 01:15:17.939
Believe it or not, that Dropbox was basically a wrapper on top of Amazon S3 storage.

01:15:18.420 --> 01:15:20.599
So Amazon built this object storage in the cloud.

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:24.340
So Dropbox came and they built a web interface on top of it.

01:15:24.460 --> 01:15:26.239
But they were storing everything in S3.

01:15:26.380 --> 01:15:30.319
And they were just charging a few more cents than Amazon S3 did.

01:15:30.599 --> 01:15:31.819
And that's how they made their money.

01:15:32.179 --> 01:15:39.840
Only later on, once they needed to scale more and the prices weren't favorable anymore,

01:15:39.840 --> 01:15:43.960
where they decide to build their own way of organizing the objects inside the distributed system.

01:15:44.100 --> 01:15:48.079
But at the beginning, Dropbox was just a wrapper on top of Amazon S3.

01:15:49.260 --> 01:15:51.440
So, very interesting way to make money.

01:15:51.760 --> 01:15:52.220
Very easy.

01:15:54.060 --> 01:15:54.380
All right.

01:15:55.460 --> 01:15:59.140
Now, as other examples of client-server applications,

01:15:59.140 --> 01:16:03.319
of course, the de facto example is the example of web servers.

01:16:03.480 --> 01:16:07.900
And here it's worth noting that web servers have changed over time.

01:16:08.479 --> 01:16:14.379
So again, 26 years ago, web servers followed a two-tier architecture

01:16:14.379 --> 01:16:16.920
where you had a client machine and a server machine.

01:16:17.239 --> 01:16:20.719
The client ran the browser, the server ran the web server.

01:16:21.199 --> 01:16:24.059
Typically, the web server was co-located with the database,

01:16:24.359 --> 01:16:25.979
so you would access the database directly.

01:16:26.859 --> 01:16:30.599
And whenever, for example, your browser would request a web page,

01:16:30.899 --> 01:16:32.359
it would send the request to the server,

01:16:32.359 --> 01:16:37.079
the server would have the web pages statically stored somewhere on the file or in the database.

01:16:37.899 --> 01:16:42.819
and we'll basically grab that HTML file, maybe with CSS, you know, stylization component,

01:16:43.000 --> 01:16:45.319
send it back to the browser, and then the browser will render.

01:16:45.559 --> 01:16:46.839
So very simple architecture.

01:16:47.679 --> 01:16:52.339
Nowadays, web servers and web applications are implemented differently.

01:16:53.219 --> 01:16:58.000
So the web pages are not stored statically on disk or a database.

01:16:58.159 --> 01:16:59.599
They're actually generated dynamically.

01:17:00.259 --> 01:17:03.439
And it makes sense because modern-day web applications are very complex.

01:17:03.799 --> 01:17:07.859
They sometimes require various kinds of data that you have to include in your web page.

01:17:07.899 --> 01:17:12.939
is dynamic data. So the best way would be to try to generate that data dynamically.

01:17:13.659 --> 01:17:19.979
So it's very common nowadays when it comes to the deployment of web servers to see multi-tiered

01:17:19.979 --> 01:17:25.099
architecture. So here is an example of a four-tier architecture. There's a box missing here. This

01:17:25.099 --> 01:17:29.739
would be the client. So the client will still send the request to a web server, but the web

01:17:29.739 --> 01:17:34.699
server does not serve the page directly now anymore. The web server actually forwards the

01:17:34.699 --> 01:17:40.199
request to a program called a CGI program or common gateway interface.

01:17:40.199 --> 01:17:47.179
You might hear the name of this gateway interface differently in practice.

01:17:47.179 --> 01:17:53.019
You know, if you do web development you might hear SGI synchronous gateway interface, ASGIA

01:17:53.019 --> 01:17:57.500
synchronous gateway interface, of course there are various advantages and events for using

01:17:57.500 --> 01:17:58.500
them.

01:17:58.500 --> 01:18:02.399
But in principle, they're all a process that will generate a webpage dynamic.

01:18:02.399 --> 01:18:07.839
And then the CGI programs might also sometimes send requests to a database that might result

01:18:07.839 --> 01:18:11.219
on a different machine to actually retrieve some parts of the data.

01:18:11.579 --> 01:18:17.859
So, this, again, makes sense if, let's say, you know, you're building the Amazon Web Store

01:18:17.859 --> 01:18:22.279
at the beginning, right, and you have a lot of information that you have to print in different

01:18:22.279 --> 01:18:26.059
formats depending on different locations, right, in the world or in different states,

01:18:26.159 --> 01:18:29.599
right, so you need to be able to access that data in a dynamic way.

01:18:29.600 --> 01:18:33.140
You might have multiple databases with multiple products, right?

01:18:33.240 --> 01:18:37.320
So you don't want to create only one web page, static web page, and then include that information

01:18:37.320 --> 01:18:38.740
there and then try to filter it out.

01:18:39.180 --> 01:18:42.800
What you want to do is you want to generate the web pages dynamically, depending on whatever

01:18:42.800 --> 01:18:48.060
the user wants to see, whatever user is searching, whatever they have in their card, so on and

01:18:48.060 --> 01:18:48.480
so forth.

01:18:49.579 --> 01:18:54.920
So it makes sense to have, again, the CGI programs that can then accommodate that kind

01:18:54.920 --> 01:18:55.920
of dynamic behavior.

01:18:55.920 --> 01:19:05.440
So the CGI program will then compile and generate an HTML page, and it will send that HTML page to the web server, which will then forward it back to the client.

01:19:08.399 --> 01:19:08.880
Okay.

01:19:10.480 --> 01:19:11.180
All right.

01:19:11.520 --> 01:19:21.739
Now, let's take a look at how do we move now from the centralized client-server model to something that will be more distributed, right?

01:19:21.739 --> 01:19:26.159
And there are various ways we can distribute these services, as we've seen so far.

01:19:26.619 --> 01:19:32.840
So there are two strategies when it comes to, let's say, if you have a layered architecture or a service-oriented architecture,

01:19:33.460 --> 01:19:41.599
you can distribute those components either by following a vertical distribution strategy or a horizontal distribution strategy.

01:19:42.239 --> 01:19:50.559
So vertical distribution strategy means that, and I'm so sorry, I keep on pressing here on the top of the screen,

01:19:50.560 --> 01:19:56.020
and for some reason it makes the screen not highlighted,

01:19:56.360 --> 01:19:58.460
but like gray sort of, and I don't know why.

01:19:58.940 --> 01:20:01.500
Anyways, so vertical distribution means that

01:20:01.500 --> 01:20:04.360
if I have some sort of distributed application,

01:20:04.560 --> 01:20:06.500
let's say organized in multiple layers,

01:20:07.280 --> 01:20:09.620
in this example is three logical layers,

01:20:10.120 --> 01:20:12.539
what I can do to make this application more distributed

01:20:12.539 --> 01:20:15.600
is I can actually run each layer on a separate machine.

01:20:16.100 --> 01:20:18.860
Why, if I have n layers, I can use n machines

01:20:18.859 --> 01:20:20.859
and I can run each layer on a separate machine.

01:20:21.000 --> 01:20:23.079
And that way, I built a distributed system.

01:20:23.880 --> 01:20:27.759
Another way would be to use the horizontal distribution strategy,

01:20:28.059 --> 01:20:30.439
which means that if I have multiple layers,

01:20:30.439 --> 01:20:32.739
instead of putting one layer on a single machine,

01:20:32.739 --> 01:20:34.500
maybe a layer is more demanding.

01:20:35.119 --> 01:20:39.460
I split that layer into two, again, cloned layers,

01:20:39.519 --> 01:20:42.279
or very similar layers, and I run them on different machines.

01:20:42.739 --> 01:20:47.659
Now, each of these layers, they have access to a separate portion of the entire data.

01:20:48.460 --> 01:20:52.479
So there's no overlap in terms of functional responsibility between those two layers.

01:20:52.920 --> 01:20:56.119
They implement the same functions, but they operate on different data sets altogether.

01:20:56.899 --> 01:21:00.680
So that's one way to, again, build another distributed system.

01:21:00.859 --> 01:21:02.800
Now, I do want to make a parenthesis here.

01:21:03.720 --> 01:21:14.159
When people talk about distributed systems, you will also hear the notion of vertical scalability and horizontal scalability.

01:21:15.159 --> 01:21:17.659
So that actually refers to something else.

01:21:18.880 --> 01:21:22.000
So when you have, let's say, an application, right,

01:21:22.059 --> 01:21:24.559
and they say, okay, does your application scale vertically

01:21:24.559 --> 01:21:26.059
or does it scale horizontally?

01:21:26.680 --> 01:21:29.800
What it refers to is does your application,

01:21:29.800 --> 01:21:32.359
does the performance of your application increase?

01:21:32.840 --> 01:21:35.639
Performance came in like how many requests per second you can process, right?

01:21:35.960 --> 01:21:40.300
Does it increase vertically if you add more cores, more RAM,

01:21:40.439 --> 01:21:42.239
more hard drives inside one single computer?

01:21:42.979 --> 01:21:44.800
So that's vertical scalability.

01:21:45.300 --> 01:21:47.979
So on one single computer, you add more resources.

01:21:48.319 --> 01:21:49.519
Does your application run past?

01:21:50.019 --> 01:21:52.979
Horizontal scalability means if I add more computers,

01:21:52.979 --> 01:21:54.939
does the application increase in performance?

01:21:55.880 --> 01:21:59.460
Okay, so when it comes to the organization of distributed systems,

01:21:59.939 --> 01:22:03.139
how do you distribute the layers of a distributed system

01:22:03.139 --> 01:22:06.699
refers to this vertical distribution versus horizontal distribution.

01:22:06.960 --> 01:22:10.000
Okay, keep in mind, again, these are differences between.

01:22:10.000 --> 01:22:13.680
All right. So again, you can split the logical layers.

01:22:13.939 --> 01:22:23.180
Or another way to organize a distributed system is by following, let's say, a peer-to-peer architecture approach.

01:22:23.180 --> 01:22:28.619
So in a peer-to-peer architecture approach, all of the components or processes that are in the system,

01:22:29.420 --> 01:22:34.239
we design them in a way where they act as both clients and servers at the same time.

01:22:34.920 --> 01:22:37.619
So all of the processes are both clients and servers,

01:22:37.880 --> 01:22:41.460
and they can contact any other process either as a client

01:22:41.460 --> 01:22:44.960
or be contacted as a server by any other process.

01:22:47.019 --> 01:22:51.439
This is the most important property of these peer-to-peer architectures.

01:22:51.659 --> 01:22:53.340
This is, again, very distributed.

01:22:54.199 --> 01:22:58.260
All of the components, they have the same role inside the distributed system.

01:22:58.260 --> 01:23:02.480
so when it comes to

01:23:02.480 --> 01:23:06.579
peer-to-peer systems there are of course

01:23:06.579 --> 01:23:10.420
two ways or two kinds of peer-to-peer systems out there

01:23:10.420 --> 01:23:14.300
so you can have structured peer-to-peer systems or unstructured

01:23:14.300 --> 01:23:18.380
peer-to-peer systems. Structured means that those

01:23:18.380 --> 01:23:22.020
again processes that act both as clients and servers

01:23:22.020 --> 01:23:26.260
they are organized in a predictable way or there is some sort of

01:23:26.260 --> 01:23:27.440
structure that they found.

01:23:28.140 --> 01:23:32.860
The unstructured means that they connect at will and each component will have different

01:23:32.900 --> 01:23:38.380
neighbors and, you know, different number of neighbors at any moment of time.

01:23:38.500 --> 01:23:42.060
They can come in, they can leave, and there is no particular structure in the way that

01:23:42.060 --> 01:23:42.659
they're organized.

01:23:43.640 --> 01:23:46.159
Now, let's look first at structured peer-to-peer networks.

01:23:46.640 --> 01:23:52.480
These are more easy to work with and they're more, let's say, popular out there.

01:23:52.479 --> 01:24:04.299
So one important challenge when it comes to implementing peer-to-peer systems actually has to do with how do you identify data inside a peer-to-peer system.

01:24:04.679 --> 01:24:09.599
In a client-server architecture, it's very easy to identify where the data is.

01:24:09.599 --> 01:24:17.819
The client can ask the server, the server can tell the client, hey, this is where the data is, or this client has the data, or I have the data.

01:24:18.059 --> 01:24:22.519
So we access a centralized entity that will tell us where the information is.

01:24:22.920 --> 01:24:25.659
But in a peer-to-peer system where any component can act,

01:24:25.719 --> 01:24:29.079
both as client and as server, we don't know exactly where the data is,

01:24:29.139 --> 01:24:32.019
especially if we want to implement some sort of data sharing,

01:24:32.259 --> 01:24:33.319
let's say peer-to-peer system.

01:24:34.920 --> 01:24:39.479
So what this means is that we need to build some sort of mechanisms

01:24:39.479 --> 01:24:42.899
to determine who has a piece of data.

01:24:43.159 --> 01:24:45.039
If you think about it, this is what Google did

01:24:45.039 --> 01:24:46.979
when they designed their Google search engine.

01:24:46.979 --> 01:24:51.459
they were trying to solve the problem of having these many servers and many clients out there

01:24:51.459 --> 01:24:56.699
that were trying to search for data, and they were trying to find out which of the servers out there,

01:24:56.799 --> 01:25:00.159
which of the web servers out there contain a piece of information.

01:25:00.519 --> 01:25:02.359
So they build an index for it.

01:25:02.819 --> 01:25:06.199
Now, the index can be built in two ways.

01:25:06.539 --> 01:25:13.039
So for structured peer-to-peer networks, because the peer-to-peer system is organized in a predictable way,

01:25:13.039 --> 01:25:16.739
we can actually build what's called a semantic free index.

01:25:16.979 --> 01:25:22.079
So let's look first at what does it mean to have a structured peer-to-peer system.

01:25:22.259 --> 01:25:24.099
So again, we can organize them.

01:25:24.159 --> 01:25:25.259
We have an example here.

01:25:26.039 --> 01:25:32.439
We can organize the, let's say, nodes or the processes in that system in a predictable way.

01:25:32.439 --> 01:25:44.739
So we can explore various kinds of graph or geometrical shapes to actually implement by the topology of the processes or of the nodes.

01:25:44.739 --> 01:25:47.679
And whenever we create

01:25:47.679 --> 01:25:49.559
this geometry or

01:25:49.559 --> 01:25:50.840
graph space

01:25:50.840 --> 01:25:53.239
that is again symmetrical

01:25:53.239 --> 01:25:55.639
or in a predictable way, we can also

01:25:55.639 --> 01:25:57.679
assign unique identifiers

01:25:58.300 --> 01:25:59.979
to all of the

01:25:59.979 --> 01:26:01.579
nodes out of the

01:26:01.579 --> 01:26:03.719
peer-to-peer system. If they are

01:26:03.719 --> 01:26:05.760
unstructured, it's very hard because

01:26:05.760 --> 01:26:07.659
we don't have a centralized way of assigning

01:26:07.659 --> 01:26:09.579
the nodes. But when it's structured,

01:26:09.719 --> 01:26:11.219
we have a way of assigning the nodes.

01:26:11.460 --> 01:26:13.639
So for the example of the

01:26:13.640 --> 01:26:19.800
hypercube let's say topology we have 16 nodes we can organize them in a hypercube configuration

01:26:19.800 --> 01:26:27.860
where basically each node has four neighbors or has four immediate immediate neighbors

01:26:27.860 --> 01:26:34.640
but they're all interconnected in a way where if a node wants to send the data to another node that

01:26:34.640 --> 01:26:38.640
is not a neighbor it can forward the data to one of the neighbors and then the other neighbor can

01:26:38.640 --> 01:26:40.600
forward it and send it to the proper node.

01:26:41.480 --> 01:26:46.560
Now also, interestingly here, in a hybrid configuration, since this is a cube in four

01:26:46.560 --> 01:26:52.280
dimensions, the way I can structure these nodes is I can give them different coordinates

01:26:52.280 --> 01:26:53.640
in that 4D space.

01:26:54.700 --> 01:27:00.600
So for example, the identification numbers that you see here for each node represents

01:27:00.600 --> 01:27:05.440
the base, let's say, vector or coordination in that 4D space.

01:27:05.439 --> 01:27:14.519
So for example, identifier 0000 means that the node resides at the center of the four-dimensional axis, right?

01:27:14.939 --> 01:27:25.419
Let's say if I have 0001, it means that it has, it's the vector with one unit in that four-dimensional space, right, on the x-axis, right?

01:27:25.439 --> 01:27:28.379
And I can arrange all of these points to create this, again, hypercube.

01:27:28.380 --> 01:27:38.359
You can also do a 3D cube if you have a smaller number of nodes, depending on how many neighbors we have.

01:27:39.260 --> 01:27:45.279
So now, because you can create these unique identifiers, and because you have a structure,

01:27:45.460 --> 01:27:48.140
you know how to forward the data between the nodes.

01:27:48.859 --> 01:27:54.020
It makes it very easy if you need to route requests, which also means that if you need to figure out a way

01:27:54.020 --> 01:27:57.920
how do you organize the data in the system so you can easily find this data,

01:27:58.380 --> 01:28:01.680
You can then use, of course, a hash function.

01:28:01.680 --> 01:28:11.000
So again, the semantic free index means that we can decide how do we organize the data in the system without knowing details about the contents of the data.

01:28:11.480 --> 01:28:13.199
And we do this by using a hash function.

01:28:13.319 --> 01:28:20.680
If you remember, the hash function, we see there's an input, a variable-sized input, and it returned a fixed-size output.

01:28:20.940 --> 01:28:23.319
But it didn't matter what I placed as an input.

01:28:23.440 --> 01:28:27.079
It could be binary data, it could be human-reliable data, it could be another number.

01:28:27.500 --> 01:28:28.300
It doesn't really matter.

01:28:28.380 --> 01:28:33.960
So whenever I want to store data inside the system, what I do is for each piece of data,

01:28:34.260 --> 01:28:37.119
I also have a key as well, right?

01:28:37.359 --> 01:28:41.699
And then for that key, I can apply a hash function, which will give me some sort of number.

01:28:41.800 --> 01:28:49.680
If I apply a module operation over the number to determine, you know, in which of these nodes the system will go,

01:28:49.920 --> 01:28:55.400
so I can do a module operation where I extract, let's say, you know, the last or the first four bits of that, you know,

01:28:55.440 --> 01:28:56.800
generated hash function number.

01:28:56.800 --> 01:29:00.539
and then by using the number I can determine which node is responsible

01:29:00.539 --> 01:29:03.220
for the piece of data that has key, let's say, k.

01:29:04.320 --> 01:29:07.320
So I store the data, right, now in this system, right,

01:29:07.360 --> 01:29:12.020
I store the data in the nodes that are responsible for managing the specific, let's say, key space.

01:29:12.779 --> 01:29:15.779
Now if I need to search for a piece of data by using the key,

01:29:15.779 --> 01:29:19.360
what I do is I calculate, right, I use the hash function to calculate, you know,

01:29:19.420 --> 01:29:23.659
which node is responsible for that data and then I send the request to that specific node.

01:29:24.519 --> 01:29:27.939
So even though all the nodes act both as clients and servers,

01:29:28.340 --> 01:29:32.319
I can still identify where the data is by building this semantic free index

01:29:32.319 --> 01:29:35.920
by exploiting the structure of the peer-to-peer network.

01:29:36.779 --> 01:29:38.659
Now, of course, this also has limitations,

01:29:38.899 --> 01:29:44.479
because so far we talked about a static, let's say, topology for this peer-to-peer network.

01:29:44.479 --> 01:29:48.139
So the number of nodes and the way that they are connected doesn't really change.

01:29:48.579 --> 01:29:52.279
But what happens if we want to remove a node or we want to add a new node?

01:29:52.279 --> 01:30:03.679
If we add a new node, we probably need to reconfigure the entire architecture, which means that probably the identification numbers for all of the nodes change or the increase in numbers.

01:30:03.840 --> 01:30:12.300
So now instead of using 4 bits, I have to use 5 bits, which means that now I have to reorganize the data and send it to the proper node because now I'm doing a modulo.

01:30:12.479 --> 01:30:16.079
Instead of, you know, the first 4 bits, I'm doing the first 5 bits instead.

01:30:16.319 --> 01:30:20.139
So I need to completely reorganize the data, which is not very efficient.

01:30:20.900 --> 01:30:23.560
So this problem, however, has been solved.

01:30:23.560 --> 01:30:30.560
So the DIM solutions, I think the Chord solution has been proposed, I think, in the early 2000s.

01:30:31.539 --> 01:30:38.420
So again, 26 years ago, people have figured out how to build a structure, peer-to-peer systems,

01:30:38.520 --> 01:30:41.460
where nodes can join and can leave as well.

01:30:42.360 --> 01:30:46.560
The way that they build this is by creating a virtual keyspace.

01:30:47.560 --> 01:30:49.640
And we're just going to discuss it again conceptually.

01:30:50.140 --> 01:30:53.500
You don't have to understand how the church system works for the exam.

01:30:54.060 --> 01:30:57.960
This will be the topic for the Distributed Systems 2 class if you want to build one of these.

01:30:58.079 --> 01:31:01.440
Or just let me know offline, and I'll give you more resources on how to build it.

01:31:01.520 --> 01:31:07.780
But what's important here is that they use this notion of the key space, or a virtual key space,

01:31:07.840 --> 01:31:15.200
and then assigning nodes to various key spaces and making them responsible for one or more key spaces.

01:31:15.199 --> 01:31:31.979
So the way that the short system works is you have to, of course, organize the nodes and give each node an mbit identifier, which data item is hashed to an mbit key.

01:31:32.880 --> 01:31:37.939
The data item with tk is stored at a node with the smallest identifier, and then the read is extended with various shortcuts.

01:31:37.939 --> 01:31:43.460
So basically, whenever you create a short system, you create first a key space.

01:31:43.460 --> 01:31:50.819
You want the keyspace to actually be larger than the number of nodes that you expect that will be part of this tracked system.

01:31:51.460 --> 01:31:57.020
So in this case, they selected the keyspace of 32 keys.

01:31:57.239 --> 01:31:59.920
So that would be 5 bits.

01:32:00.260 --> 01:32:03.439
So they used 5 bits to create a keyspace, right?

01:32:03.500 --> 01:32:05.579
You can have 32 unique keys.

01:32:05.819 --> 01:32:10.279
2 to the power of 5 is 32 elements, so 32 unique keys.

01:32:10.899 --> 01:32:14.460
So a piece of data can be placed in any of these keyspaces.

01:32:14.779 --> 01:32:16.340
These are, again, are virtual keyspaces.

01:32:16.479 --> 01:32:20.159
Now, whenever a node joins the CHUR network,

01:32:20.479 --> 01:32:22.699
they will be assigned an identifier.

01:32:23.399 --> 01:32:25.960
The identifier is the same as the keyspace.

01:32:26.880 --> 01:32:29.939
And the node will then be responsible

01:32:29.939 --> 01:32:34.539
to manage all of the keys that are stored

01:32:34.579 --> 01:32:36.979
in all of the key spaces

01:32:36.979 --> 01:32:42.819
that are less or equal than the identifier until they reach a new node.

01:32:43.039 --> 01:32:46.739
So for example here, let's say that I have, in this diagram,

01:32:46.939 --> 01:32:49.299
so the dotted line represents a key space.

01:32:50.159 --> 01:32:54.779
The full line or full circle represents a node

01:32:54.779 --> 01:32:58.359
that is also managing the key space with that same number.

01:32:58.479 --> 01:33:01.099
So in this case, let's say that I have node number 9.

01:33:01.439 --> 01:33:05.579
Node number 9 is responsible for managing all of the data

01:33:05.579 --> 01:33:09.300
that goes into key space nine, eight, seven, six, and five.

01:33:10.739 --> 01:33:13.220
For example, node number four is responsible

01:33:13.220 --> 01:33:17.059
for all of the data that falls into key space four, three, and two,

01:33:17.899 --> 01:33:20.140
and so on and so forth, right?

01:33:20.199 --> 01:33:23.819
So it means that when I want to insert a piece of data in the system,

01:33:24.399 --> 01:33:26.539
I have to calculate the hash function,

01:33:26.539 --> 01:33:31.659
and I have to see which node is responsible with managing that data.

01:33:32.119 --> 01:33:35.039
Again, in this system, the nodes are also the client,

01:33:35.039 --> 01:33:39.300
So they're the ones who are initiating these operations to figure out where do I store the data.

01:33:40.539 --> 01:33:47.779
If I want to do a lookup for the data, let's say that, you know, in this example, they do a lookup from node 9,

01:33:48.060 --> 01:33:54.220
and they're searching for a piece of data that has, let's say, the hash function applied over the key is key 3,

01:33:54.539 --> 01:33:58.899
so the data will be in key space number 3.

01:33:59.019 --> 01:34:03.220
We know that key space number 3 is managed by node 4, right?

01:34:03.220 --> 01:34:10.699
So again, there are a few shortcuts that get created and there is a specific rule on how you create the shortcuts in the charge system.

01:34:11.199 --> 01:34:18.020
But here, and then after you create the shortcuts, the rule is that you want to go to the highest node that can manage that key.

01:34:18.140 --> 01:34:27.460
So in this case, node number nine will forward request to node 28 because that's the highest number that can maybe manage their key.

01:34:28.159 --> 01:34:33.939
Then from here, from 28, it jumps to node 1 and it jumps to node 4.

01:34:34.100 --> 01:34:37.060
And then node 4 is the one that manages key space 2.

01:34:39.140 --> 01:34:43.159
So again, by using the shortcuts, you can find the data.

01:34:43.279 --> 01:34:50.060
Now, the beauty of this system is that you can add new nodes without having to reorganize the entire data.

01:34:50.199 --> 01:34:56.560
So let's say that I add a new node and the new node maybe receives identifier 7,

01:34:56.560 --> 01:35:01.039
which means that now the node has to be responsible for the keys 7, 6, and 5.

01:35:01.300 --> 01:35:05.280
So when the new node gets added, the shortcuts get updated.

01:35:06.060 --> 01:35:10.900
And then node number 7 will let its successor know that,

01:35:11.020 --> 01:35:16.320
hey, I'm going to be managing from now on all of the keys in key space 7, 6, and 5.

01:35:16.800 --> 01:35:21.420
So node number 9 will just transfer the data from key space 7, 6, and 5.

01:35:21.620 --> 01:35:25.320
And then node number 9 will only be responsible with key spaces 9 in.

01:35:25.319 --> 01:35:32.439
right so this is a better way to reorganize the data we have without having to reconvert you know

01:35:32.439 --> 01:35:36.859
and we transfer all the data between all of the nodes we just transfer the data between the two

01:35:36.859 --> 01:35:41.819
nodes if for example in the seem architecture node number nine wants to live it needs to contact

01:35:41.819 --> 01:35:48.319
its successor and say hey i am going to leave here is all of the key spaces that i manage all of the

01:35:48.319 --> 01:35:52.359
data from the key spaces that i manage you're now responsible with managing these key spaces

01:35:52.359 --> 01:35:57.359
So, we're just transferring the data from 9 to 11, and 11 will then manage all of the data.

01:35:58.019 --> 01:36:02.179
So, again, examples on how you can build structured peer-to-peer networks.

01:36:02.299 --> 01:36:04.639
So, again, this example is old.

01:36:05.219 --> 01:36:10.899
However, this example is a precursor of a more modern technique that people use

01:36:10.899 --> 01:36:15.339
to organize data in a distributed system, especially with structured peer-to-peer networks.

01:36:15.819 --> 01:36:19.460
So, the most popular solution is a distributed hash table.

01:36:19.460 --> 01:36:28.359
Because this kind of works like a hash table where you have a key space and you're hashing the data and you're placing it in a specific bucket.

01:36:29.020 --> 01:36:32.119
And you make certain nodes be responsible for certain buckets.

01:36:32.439 --> 01:36:35.380
Now, how do you determine which node is responsible for which bucket?

01:36:35.779 --> 01:36:37.399
Well, you create another hash table.

01:36:37.520 --> 01:36:39.100
So you have a hierarchical hash table.

01:36:39.220 --> 01:36:42.439
The first hash table tells you which node manages the data.

01:36:42.439 --> 01:36:44.460
and then the second hash table tells you

01:36:44.460 --> 01:36:47.659
in which part of that node you can find the data

01:36:47.659 --> 01:36:49.819
or in which button you can find the data.

01:36:50.719 --> 01:36:53.419
And this again has an extra hub,

01:36:53.500 --> 01:36:57.219
but there's also another kind of data structure out there

01:36:57.219 --> 01:36:59.179
that can help you build these peer-to-peer systems,

01:36:59.179 --> 01:37:02.019
which is called a zero-hub distributed hash table.

01:37:02.460 --> 01:37:04.659
And that's the most recent, let's say, discovery

01:37:04.659 --> 01:37:07.939
when it comes to organizing peer-to-peer argumentators.

01:37:10.319 --> 01:37:10.639
All right.

01:37:10.640 --> 01:37:14.539
But what happens if you cannot structure a peer-to-peer architecture?

01:37:14.660 --> 01:37:17.579
That can happen when you build a peer-to-peer system over the Internet.

01:37:18.060 --> 01:37:20.619
There's no guarantee that you can build it in a structured way.

01:37:20.980 --> 01:37:25.400
Usually structured peer-to-peer networks, they're still built in a tightly clustered system

01:37:25.400 --> 01:37:28.160
or tightly connected system that you have control over.

01:37:28.260 --> 01:37:29.820
You can build some sort of topology.

01:37:30.700 --> 01:37:32.480
So for unstructured peer-to-peer networks,

01:37:32.940 --> 01:37:37.539
unfortunately we are at the mercy of just when we need to search for a piece of data,

01:37:37.539 --> 01:37:44.260
we are at the mercy of flooding the network with messages to see whoever has that piece of data.

01:37:44.779 --> 01:37:46.859
So this strategy, again, is for flooding.

01:37:47.239 --> 01:37:52.640
Basically, when you need to search for a piece of data, you send a request to all of your neighbors.

01:37:53.340 --> 01:37:55.539
The neighbors also send a request to their neighbors,

01:37:55.859 --> 01:37:58.960
and you just end up flooding the network in order to be able to find the resource.

01:37:59.180 --> 01:38:03.899
This is not very efficient, especially from a network bandwidth point of view.

01:38:03.899 --> 01:38:07.819
If everyone wants to search, the entire network will be flooded with messages.

01:38:08.739 --> 01:38:09.759
It's not very efficient.

01:38:10.479 --> 01:38:17.139
One important property is whenever you send these messages, you have to also put a time-to-live, let's say, parameter program,

01:38:17.539 --> 01:38:19.939
so that they don't stay there in the network indefinitely.

01:38:20.179 --> 01:38:21.079
The data cannot be found.

01:38:21.719 --> 01:38:25.939
You want to be able at some point for them to just die, which means you might never receive an answer.

01:38:25.939 --> 01:38:36.619
So another solution, especially for unstructured 3D networks where the data is replicated so there are many copies of the data, you can also do a random walk.

01:38:36.719 --> 01:38:46.339
So a random walk means that you're sending that search message to a subset of your neighbors and then they forward that message to a subset of their neighbors.

01:38:46.919 --> 01:38:50.439
And at some point, since the data is replicated, you will hit it at some point.

01:38:50.539 --> 01:38:51.500
There's a higher chance of you.

01:38:51.500 --> 01:38:58.760
However, the practical way that people solve this problem of finding resources in unstructured

01:38:58.760 --> 01:39:02.539
peer-to-peer networks is to actually build a superpeer network.

01:39:03.779 --> 01:39:10.260
So a superpeer network, in a superpeer network, you still have a bunch of peers that are organized

01:39:10.260 --> 01:39:11.520
in an unstructured way.

01:39:11.520 --> 01:39:13.680
We'll call these peers weak peers.

01:39:14.319 --> 01:39:19.899
And then on top of these weak peers, we can then assign or we can build a set of superpeers.

01:39:20.739 --> 01:39:25.159
Now, the weak peers, they are organized in a, again, unstructured way.

01:39:25.619 --> 01:39:31.519
As long as a weak peer can connect to one super peer, then it can function inside the network.

01:39:31.979 --> 01:39:35.719
Even if it doesn't connect directly to the super peer, it can still function.

01:39:35.879 --> 01:39:40.839
Now, the super peers are typically organized in a structured way.

01:39:41.379 --> 01:39:44.439
And they create what's called an overlay network.

01:39:45.259 --> 01:39:47.579
So they are organized in a structured way.

01:39:47.579 --> 01:39:54.939
And these super peers, they act as indexing servers for the data that is stored in this super peer network.

01:39:55.720 --> 01:39:58.420
The data resides actually on the weaker peers.

01:39:58.920 --> 01:40:04.180
But the weaker peers, they communicate with the super peer, and the super peer collects an index of the data.

01:40:05.140 --> 01:40:10.279
Imagine that you were to take your application to the next level in programming assignment 2.

01:40:10.279 --> 01:40:17.180
And let's say that you want to implement an application that allows you to index data not only on your computer,

01:40:17.180 --> 01:40:20.960
but across on every student's computer in this class, right?

01:40:21.100 --> 01:40:22.400
We can implement it in two ways.

01:40:22.520 --> 01:40:23.960
We can build a centralized server

01:40:23.960 --> 01:40:25.000
where we build the index

01:40:25.000 --> 01:40:27.060
and everyone will upload their files

01:40:27.060 --> 01:40:29.320
or index their files and update it in the server,

01:40:29.600 --> 01:40:32.320
or we can implement it in a peer-to-peer fashion

01:40:32.320 --> 01:40:34.440
where everyone, right,

01:40:34.600 --> 01:40:36.680
stores a local copy of the index.

01:40:37.020 --> 01:40:38.240
Now, there are two ways we can do this.

01:40:38.300 --> 01:40:39.320
We can do it structurally

01:40:39.320 --> 01:40:41.320
where we assign unique identifiers

01:40:41.320 --> 01:40:42.140
all of the computers

01:40:42.140 --> 01:40:43.360
and then move the data

01:40:43.360 --> 01:40:46.039
that belongs to one identifier or key space

01:40:46.039 --> 01:40:50.000
on the different computers, or we can do it in an unstructured way

01:40:50.000 --> 01:40:51.619
where you have to communicate with each other.

01:40:52.340 --> 01:40:54.800
Ideally, what you would like to do is you would like to have

01:40:54.800 --> 01:40:57.680
a few of the colleagues act as superpeers that you can contact

01:40:57.680 --> 01:40:59.340
to find where the data is.

01:41:00.199 --> 01:41:01.960
This is how the superpeer network works.

01:41:03.979 --> 01:41:07.960
Now, let's see an example of a system that implements

01:41:07.960 --> 01:41:09.079
a superpeer network.

01:41:09.380 --> 01:41:12.359
And, of course, we go back to the BitTorrent things.

01:41:13.220 --> 01:41:15.920
And I've already mentioned right before that the BitTorrent protocol

01:41:15.920 --> 01:41:22.039
in order to overcome the problems of this asymmetry that we have on the internet.

01:41:22.220 --> 01:41:26.180
The fact that we can download at fast speed, but we can upload them at most speeds.

01:41:26.659 --> 01:41:31.699
So to be able to download data from other peers without using a data center or a server,

01:41:32.180 --> 01:41:36.659
it would be better to split the data into multiple chunks and then download it from multiple people.

01:41:36.859 --> 01:41:40.300
That way, if we download a chunk at 20 megabits per second,

01:41:40.619 --> 01:41:44.380
if I download five chunks from five different people, I can get 100 megabits per second downloaded.

01:41:45.300 --> 01:41:47.960
Okay, so that was the main problem that they were trying to solve.

01:41:48.560 --> 01:41:52.779
Now, the BitTorrent protocol, it's organized as a super-peer architecture.

01:41:53.760 --> 01:41:58.940
So the nodes, or the weak peers, are the nodes that contain the actual data.

01:41:59.380 --> 01:42:01.560
The data gets split into multiple chunks.

01:42:03.159 --> 01:42:10.119
Now, the super-peers are named trackers in the BitTorrent protocol.

01:42:10.640 --> 01:42:14.539
So a tracker is another server that keeps track of the peers

01:42:14.539 --> 01:42:16.059
and what kind of data they have.

01:42:17.140 --> 01:42:25.340
Now what a tracker can do, a tracker can be added to a file called a torrent file.

01:42:25.579 --> 01:42:28.300
A torrent file, basically, it's another type of index

01:42:28.300 --> 01:42:30.439
where according to the data that you're looking for,

01:42:30.720 --> 01:42:33.000
it will tell you which trackers might have that data.

01:42:34.140 --> 01:42:39.559
Okay, so let's say if you're running the BitTorrent,

01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:43.080
you know, client or program on your computer, if you want to download

01:42:43.080 --> 01:42:46.740
a piece of data, you would first need to go and access

01:42:46.740 --> 01:42:50.780
a web server to find the trackers

01:42:50.780 --> 01:42:55.260
that can help you download that data. So the web server, from the web

01:42:55.260 --> 01:42:59.080
server, you typically download some sort of torrent file. The torrent file will have a

01:42:59.080 --> 01:43:00.120
number of trackers.

01:43:02.780 --> 01:43:05.280
After you extract the trackers, your

01:43:05.280 --> 01:43:09.060
BitTorrent program will contact the trackers and

01:43:09.060 --> 01:43:13.120
will try to join the swarm as long as the data resides there.

01:43:13.520 --> 01:43:16.100
So in the BitTorrent protocol, in order to be able to download

01:43:16.100 --> 01:43:18.980
a piece of data, you have to join a swarm of nodes.

01:43:19.480 --> 01:43:22.800
Those nodes create a swarm when they want to share

01:43:22.800 --> 01:43:23.820
the same piece of data.

01:43:24.820 --> 01:43:27.240
So when you join the swarm for the first time,

01:43:27.600 --> 01:43:32.600
the swarm will give you a few chunks of data for free.

01:43:33.160 --> 01:43:37.200
So the BitTorrent protocol tried to also solve the problem

01:43:37.199 --> 01:43:39.199
of people who would abuse the system,

01:43:39.319 --> 01:43:40.619
who would only want to download

01:43:40.619 --> 01:43:42.559
but not share data, right?

01:43:42.579 --> 01:43:44.139
In order for the system to work well,

01:43:44.500 --> 01:43:45.500
people have to, you know,

01:43:45.559 --> 01:43:47.139
their systems have to be able

01:43:47.139 --> 01:43:47.819
to share data

01:43:47.819 --> 01:43:49.519
but also download data at the same time.

01:43:50.019 --> 01:43:52.220
So in order to balance that out,

01:43:53.059 --> 01:43:54.099
inside a swarm,

01:43:54.159 --> 01:43:54.880
when you want to download

01:43:54.880 --> 01:43:55.579
the piece of data,

01:43:55.760 --> 01:43:57.279
you receive a few chunks for free,

01:43:57.720 --> 01:43:59.119
but then to download more chunks,

01:43:59.179 --> 01:44:01.079
you have to share your chunks as well.

01:44:01.479 --> 01:44:03.380
So you can only download once,

01:44:03.460 --> 01:44:04.179
you know, other people

01:44:04.179 --> 01:44:05.399
want that data as well.

01:44:06.039 --> 01:44:09.420
So once you join the swarm, again, you get a few chunks,

01:44:09.420 --> 01:44:13.879
and then you start sharing and downloading out of chunks until you have your company there.

01:44:14.500 --> 01:44:18.460
If you ever used the BitTorrent programs before,

01:44:19.019 --> 01:44:23.420
you might have noticed sometimes that you would download a file and it would stop like 80%.

01:44:24.059 --> 01:44:27.359
It doesn't necessarily mean that those computers were down.

01:44:27.779 --> 01:44:32.439
It might also mean that maybe you don't have enough computers inside a swarm that have those missing chunks.

01:44:33.299 --> 01:44:37.099
Or it means that maybe your computer was not able to share enough chunks

01:44:37.099 --> 01:44:38.759
with other nodes as well,

01:44:39.599 --> 01:44:42.919
because all of those nodes were already replicated on other machines.

01:44:43.699 --> 01:44:46.339
Okay, but anyways, this is how the bTorrent protocol works.

01:44:46.339 --> 01:44:52.639
So, the tracker acts as a superpeer that basically can detect other nodes

01:44:52.639 --> 01:44:54.919
and keeps track of other nodes that contain files.

01:44:55.759 --> 01:44:59.919
And then we contact those trackers by extracting them from a Torrent file

01:44:59.919 --> 01:45:01.359
that is built by other nodes.

01:45:01.359 --> 01:45:14.819
So by having this again multi-layered architecture in a sense, it was very hard for people to blame on the companies that were maintaining the web servers.

01:45:14.979 --> 01:45:18.979
Because the web servers, they were just maintaining the torrent files of the trackers.

01:45:19.039 --> 01:45:20.579
They were not storing any kind of data.

01:45:20.579 --> 01:45:26.739
So again, we know that the BitTorrent is used to transfer data illegally, especially copyrighted

01:45:26.739 --> 01:45:27.739
data.

01:45:27.739 --> 01:45:33.119
But because the service where you find the torrent files, it's very hard to prosecute

01:45:33.119 --> 01:45:36.039
the web servers because they don't store the data itself.

01:45:36.039 --> 01:45:40.800
They just store information about the tracker that actually keep track of the nodes that

01:45:40.800 --> 01:45:41.800
actually have the data.

01:45:41.800 --> 01:45:44.319
So it's very hard to figure out who's responsible.

01:45:44.319 --> 01:45:46.319
But they move the responsibility now to the users.

01:45:46.319 --> 01:45:50.380
So now if somebody is downloading that data, they're at fault, not the website.

