especially around holidays, Black Friday, Cyber Monday, there was a spike in requests.

02:08:32.239 --> 02:08:34.159
A lot of people were buying a lot of things.

02:08:34.680 --> 02:08:37.159
It was stressing their infrastructure.

02:08:37.399 --> 02:08:42.359
Basically, in order to be able to satisfy an increasing number of requests, buy more hardware,

02:08:42.819 --> 02:08:43.440
which they did.

02:08:43.659 --> 02:08:46.159
According to Jeff Bezos, let's build it.

02:08:46.260 --> 02:08:49.880
We want everyone to be able to buy stuff, especially during the holidays.

02:08:49.880 --> 02:08:56.300
But then the question was, what do we do with those computers during regular time where the requests go low?

02:08:56.460 --> 02:08:59.720
You know, they can decrease by orders of magnitude, right?

02:08:59.779 --> 02:09:01.300
So what do we do with those computers?

02:09:01.300 --> 02:09:04.440
Because, you know, if you keep them on, I mean, they waste energy.

02:09:04.920 --> 02:09:07.180
If you turn them off, they still depreciate, right?

02:09:07.480 --> 02:09:09.220
You still need to do maintenance over them.

02:09:09.260 --> 02:09:10.420
They still break over time.

02:09:11.060 --> 02:09:15.640
For certain components, for example, for SSDs, you have to connect them to electricity.

02:09:15.640 --> 02:09:20.160
Otherwise, your data gets lost or the internal systems get corrupted over time.

02:09:20.860 --> 02:09:23.119
So they have to be used.

02:09:23.240 --> 02:09:30.020
So then that's where they thought about, okay, what if we can maybe share these computers with people that need it?

02:09:30.280 --> 02:09:34.340
And they can rent these computers out for a shorter period of time.

02:09:34.760 --> 02:09:38.039
So this started intersecting with this notion of utility computing.

02:09:38.039 --> 02:09:48.399
And in the end, what allowed Amazon to build their Amazon AWS, you know, cloud services was actually also a very important piece of technology called virtualization.

02:09:49.340 --> 02:10:02.600
So virtualization, right, is this technology that allows you to actually abstract and carve out parts of a computer by creating virtual components of a real computer, right?

02:10:02.600 --> 02:10:08.680
So, if we look here, the first layer of a cloud system is always going to be the hardware system.

02:10:10.060 --> 02:10:12.000
What else are doing right now?

02:10:13.100 --> 02:10:15.680
Anyways, the first layer is the hardware, right?

02:10:15.680 --> 02:10:22.700
So, this is defined by the computers that are part of the data center or data centers that make up a cloud system.

02:10:23.180 --> 02:10:25.740
And the computers, they provide some basic resources.

02:10:25.900 --> 02:10:26.820
They provide CPU.

02:10:27.100 --> 02:10:29.140
They provide memory, disk, network.

02:10:29.320 --> 02:10:30.880
These are the very simple resources.

02:10:31.440 --> 02:10:36.100
Now, for somebody who has a startup and they want to build an application,

02:10:36.380 --> 02:10:39.659
they need these kinds of resources in order to test out their applications.

02:10:40.560 --> 02:10:44.119
Now, one way is that you can rent out a physical computer if you want,

02:10:44.460 --> 02:10:47.319
but a lot of the time people don't need a very large computer.

02:10:47.480 --> 02:10:48.779
Maybe they need something smaller.

02:10:49.000 --> 02:10:54.279
Maybe they just need something that just emulates an Android phone or a 100 iOS phones.

02:10:54.420 --> 02:10:57.319
They can run it in separate virtual machines, let's say.

02:10:57.319 --> 02:11:16.779
So this is where virtualization allowed them to then build this, you know, system that, you know, could give people, you know, a part of a computer, a small part of a computer with, you know, they can specify how many cores, how much RAM, how much storage, what kind of network, you know, capabilities do you want on those machines, right?

02:11:16.779 --> 02:11:19.979
And they can provide these as a resource, as a continuous resource.

02:11:20.099 --> 02:11:25.899
So if somebody wants to use it for a short amount of time to run an experiment, let's say for three days, they can do that, right?

02:11:25.899 --> 02:11:30.859
And then they can shut off these VMs, and they only have to pay for the amount of time that they use those machines.

02:11:31.359 --> 02:11:39.719
Now, of course, when it comes to cloud computing systems versus self-hosted architecture, right, there's always the question, you know, which one is better, which one is cheaper?

02:11:39.720 --> 02:11:48.699
The question usually gets answered once you do an analysis to see over how long of a period of time you're going to use the system.

02:11:49.140 --> 02:12:01.140
If you just need, for example, and you have another idea and you need an AI model and you want to train an AI model, but you need a powerful workstation to do that, you can buy the workstation, which will be very expensive.

02:12:01.140 --> 02:12:06.200
or you can rent if your AMR only takes, you know, let's say a day to run in the cloud,

02:12:06.560 --> 02:12:09.420
you probably just rent the machine in the cloud, right?

02:12:09.460 --> 02:12:13.820
It would be way cheaper than buying the actual infrastructure just to train for one day.

02:12:14.680 --> 02:12:21.520
Now, if you know your application will need to run this training for a very long period of time, right,

02:12:21.579 --> 02:12:24.820
it might make more sense to buy your own infrastructure and then it will be cheaper.

02:12:25.600 --> 02:12:26.260
But it depends.

02:12:26.260 --> 02:12:31.520
So you need to make this, in a sense, analysis to see for a certain application in business, you know,

02:12:31.780 --> 02:12:36.699
does it make more sense to use resources from the cloud or does it make more sense to buy your own infrastructure?

02:12:37.199 --> 02:12:44.039
Unfortunately, I think right now the trends, especially with, you know, the increasing price in memory,

02:12:44.720 --> 02:12:50.640
I think it makes it very easy for the cloud vendors to actually buy a lot of the RAM,

02:12:50.640 --> 02:12:57.140
buy a lot of the computing systems, and then make it so that, you know, for regular users to buy a server,

02:12:57.260 --> 02:13:00.460
it's going to become more expensive than to actually rent it in the cloud,

02:13:01.060 --> 02:13:04.920
which is kind of unfortunate because if you have access to the physical system,

02:13:05.020 --> 02:13:09.579
especially from a learning perspective, you end up learning more than just using something in the cloud.

02:13:10.520 --> 02:13:16.060
Anyways, so using this technology, it allowed them to build the first layer

02:13:16.060 --> 02:13:19.080
that resides in cloud computing systems,

02:13:19.180 --> 02:13:20.840
which is the infrastructure layer.

02:13:21.300 --> 02:13:22.800
So again, cloud computing systems,

02:13:22.940 --> 02:13:24.620
they're organized as layers, right?

02:13:24.640 --> 02:13:26.100
We can use the layering technique

02:13:26.100 --> 02:13:29.140
to actually understand how they're organizing structure.

02:13:29.700 --> 02:13:32.200
So the infrastructure layer provides

02:13:32.200 --> 02:13:35.060
what they call in cloud computing systems,

02:13:35.180 --> 02:13:37.060
infrastructure as a service.

02:13:38.300 --> 02:13:40.300
So this means that they allow you

02:13:40.300 --> 02:13:42.860
to create virtual machines with virtual processors,

02:13:43.160 --> 02:13:45.440
virtual memory, virtual disk, virtual network.

02:13:45.439 --> 02:13:50.819
You can also create network storage devices that can be attached to your virtual machines if you want to.

02:13:51.259 --> 02:13:54.939
You can create, of course, you can use objects for a system, so on and so forth.

02:13:55.419 --> 02:14:02.279
So this is the kind of resources that you get whenever you access a cloud, either a public cloud or a private cloud.

02:14:02.639 --> 02:14:05.960
It allows you to create virtual machines that reside on those computers.

02:14:06.479 --> 02:14:12.839
Now, of course, the price of those virtual machines, they change according to the supply and demand, right?

02:14:12.840 --> 02:14:15.460
So Amazon has a certain supply of servers, right?

02:14:15.739 --> 02:14:19.600
And depending on how many people are coming and buying, they can increase or decrease the prices.

02:14:20.699 --> 02:14:29.480
You know, it might be worth studying to see what happens with those prices when they have those spikes, you know, during Black Friday, during, you know, Cyber Monday, when people buy a lot of stuff, right?

02:14:30.600 --> 02:14:34.640
It would be interesting to see, you know, how the prices change for the games.

02:14:34.800 --> 02:14:41.060
But generally speaking, right, again, they can change depending on how many users they have.

02:14:42.840 --> 02:14:49.100
All right, so this is the first layer, which provides, again, basically virtual machines.

02:14:49.300 --> 02:14:53.440
And we know that virtual machines are software representation of hardware.

02:14:53.720 --> 02:14:57.260
So this means that you can run operating systems on them, you can install compilers.

02:14:57.260 --> 02:15:02.560
So if anyone works on a project, they can go use that resource, set up their system whichever way they want.

02:15:02.640 --> 02:15:04.579
They get root permission on the machine.

02:15:04.739 --> 02:15:08.020
They can install packages, set it up, whichever way they want.

02:15:08.020 --> 02:15:16.800
Now, I just want to mention as well that most public clouds out there, including Amazon AWS, Google Cloud Platform, Microsoft Azure,

02:15:17.300 --> 02:15:20.880
they usually give you virtual machines as resources.

02:15:21.280 --> 02:15:24.680
But there exist other clouds that can also give you access to bare metal machines.

02:15:24.680 --> 02:15:33.040
And actually, Chameleon Cloud, which we will be using in this class, actually, it's a bare metal cloud that provides you access to a physical machine.

02:15:33.039 --> 02:15:37.439
and they use a different mechanism to make sure that people don't abuse the systems and that they

02:15:37.439 --> 02:15:42.720
use the resources they might fully and we'll see you once we introduce comedian cloud and we'll

02:15:42.720 --> 02:15:47.760
see how that works now one thing that people have observed when people started building these clouds

02:15:47.760 --> 02:15:53.359
and using these virtual machines that were running in the clouds is sometimes for you know a software

02:15:53.359 --> 02:15:58.880
developer is they didn't necessarily care about what operating system was running on that vm which

02:15:58.880 --> 02:16:00.340
version of the compiler.

02:16:00.680 --> 02:16:02.279
They didn't really care about those details.

02:16:02.699 --> 02:16:05.300
What did matter for them is that, hey, I'm building a web application.

02:16:05.500 --> 02:16:07.199
I just need a database and a web server.

02:16:07.760 --> 02:16:08.800
That's all that I need.

02:16:08.800 --> 02:16:10.819
I don't need to know

02:16:10.819 --> 02:16:12.640
what operating system is, the database

02:16:12.640 --> 02:16:14.699
running on, when it was last updated,

02:16:14.760 --> 02:16:15.840
I don't really care about it.

02:16:16.319 --> 02:16:18.600
So this now introduces us to another layer

02:16:18.600 --> 02:16:20.600
that exists inside CloudContinue Systems,

02:16:20.819 --> 02:16:22.159
which is the platform layer.

02:16:22.480 --> 02:16:24.300
Or the layer that provides

02:16:24.300 --> 02:16:26.779
platform as a service services.

02:16:26.779 --> 02:16:45.299
So in this layer, people who are designing cloud systems, they realize that people sometimes maybe just need access to a database that sits in an isolated environment. Maybe sometimes they need access to a web server. Maybe sometimes they need access to some sort of big data computational framework like Hadoop or Spark.

02:16:46.039 --> 02:16:47.279
That's all that they needed.

02:16:47.279 --> 02:16:52.199
They didn't really need an actual VM where they had to set up a network,

02:16:52.399 --> 02:16:54.239
the users, they didn't really care about it.

02:16:54.279 --> 02:16:58.259
They just wanted a place where they were able to run applications and test applications.

02:16:58.859 --> 02:17:03.559
So they started providing different kinds of platform-as-a-service services,

02:17:03.679 --> 02:17:09.079
such as Database as a Service, Hadoop as a Service, Web Server as a Service.

02:17:09.299 --> 02:17:12.819
Now you can just create these services instead of spawning VMs.

02:17:12.819 --> 02:17:19.239
What's interesting is that, of course, the platform layer makes use of the layer below.

02:17:19.739 --> 02:17:24.079
So all of these platforms are actually created on top of virtual machines

02:17:24.079 --> 02:17:26.340
that are set up in the infrastructure layer.

02:17:26.440 --> 02:17:29.199
And the infrastructure layer makes use of the hardware underneath it.

02:17:29.219 --> 02:17:31.579
They're all deployed on the physical machines.

02:17:32.440 --> 02:17:36.799
And then lastly, they also realize that people maybe sometimes

02:17:36.799 --> 02:17:39.180
they just need access to certain applications,

02:17:39.180 --> 02:17:42.459
like word processing applications or spreadsheets.

02:17:42.819 --> 02:17:46.860
Maybe they just want to watch, you know, videos on YouTube or on Netflix.

02:17:47.020 --> 02:17:51.219
So they also built another layer that sits on top of the platforms layer,

02:17:51.279 --> 02:17:52.539
which is the application layer.

02:17:52.899 --> 02:17:56.879
And this is the layer that provides software as a service, let's say services.

02:17:57.440 --> 02:18:00.340
So it allows you to basically run different kind of apps.

02:18:00.340 --> 02:18:02.719
Now, if I were to ask you, you know,

02:18:02.920 --> 02:18:06.539
if you've had a chance to use any of these AI tools like chat GPT,

02:18:07.059 --> 02:18:09.440
you know, in which layer would you say that, you know,

02:18:09.459 --> 02:18:11.199
systems like chat GPT fall?

02:18:11.200 --> 02:18:15.380
because in the application there are platforms, infrastructure, and hardware there.

02:18:17.620 --> 02:18:24.880
It depends on the implementation, but, you know, if you were to throw a guess, you know, where might it be?

02:18:25.860 --> 02:18:29.200
So application, most of the times, especially if you just use the chat,

02:18:29.600 --> 02:18:33.400
I know that they also created these, you know, agent development stuff,

02:18:33.500 --> 02:18:36.140
so that could be, you know, in the platform area,

02:18:36.260 --> 02:18:39.800
because now it's spawning other agents, doing other computation, right?

02:18:39.799 --> 02:18:44.199
It's using no resources, you can create containers where you can test the application, right?

02:18:44.299 --> 02:18:49.199
So depending on how you use these AI tools, if you just use the prompt, you know, it's in the application side.

02:18:49.199 --> 02:18:55.099
But if you use these advanced, you know, agentic tools, you can go in the platform area as well.

02:18:56.799 --> 02:18:57.099
All right.

02:18:57.420 --> 02:19:01.579
But again, the most important aspect is that, again, cloud computing systems, even though they're very complex,

02:19:02.179 --> 02:19:03.879
they might use various kinds of layers.

02:19:04.239 --> 02:19:07.599
Or right here, you might have, for example, the infrastructure layer might be implemented,

02:19:07.979 --> 02:19:09.479
maybe using a peer-to-peer system,

02:19:09.600 --> 02:19:11.680
maybe using a publisher-subscriber, who knows.

02:19:12.040 --> 02:19:14.960
But you can understand that it's the higher-level layers

02:19:14.960 --> 02:19:18.479
by using the layer in, let's say, a approach.

