02:19:29.180 --> 02:19:32.180
when we talked about introduction.

02:19:33.239 --> 02:19:35.760
There was a discussion about edge computing systems.

02:19:36.280 --> 02:19:41.360
So just to understand, so once cloud computing systems became, you know, more and more popular,

02:19:41.840 --> 02:19:45.860
people realized that they can offset computation from edge devices.

02:19:46.280 --> 02:19:52.540
Edge devices could mean, you know, sensors, you know, smart TVs, smart, I don't know, refrigerators.

02:19:52.540 --> 02:19:54.540
It also means smartphones as well.

02:19:54.840 --> 02:19:58.340
They can move the heavy computation from the device itself to the cloud.

02:19:58.639 --> 02:20:02.440
So, right, for example, if you wanted to have translating capabilities on your phone,

02:20:02.440 --> 02:20:04.420
instead of, you know, putting, you know,

02:20:04.520 --> 02:20:06.780
a particle processor on your phone and more RAM

02:20:06.780 --> 02:20:08.540
and more computation capabilities,

02:20:08.540 --> 02:20:10.180
which made the phone very expensive,

02:20:10.720 --> 02:20:12.399
you could offload actually the computation

02:20:12.399 --> 02:20:13.600
in the cloud and do it there.

02:20:13.659 --> 02:20:15.280
So that's how Google Translate works

02:20:15.280 --> 02:20:16.800
or Google Lens, right?

02:20:16.819 --> 02:20:17.720
Whenever you use Translate,

02:20:17.819 --> 02:20:19.880
it actually gets sent somewhere in the cloud

02:20:19.880 --> 02:20:21.260
and this is a translation for you.

02:20:21.260 --> 02:20:24.200
You can still download, I think, models locally

02:20:24.200 --> 02:20:25.300
because now they use AI.

02:20:25.460 --> 02:20:26.600
They can compress the models,

02:20:27.020 --> 02:20:28.860
but they're more powerful when you use them in the cloud.

02:20:29.420 --> 02:20:31.140
And then for Google Lens, you have to use cloud

02:20:31.139 --> 02:20:34.739
because there's image processing that has to be done to convert, for example,

02:20:34.859 --> 02:20:37.239
a text from one language to the other that you can see on your phone.

02:20:37.699 --> 02:20:39.340
So this is typically done in the cloud.

02:20:39.340 --> 02:20:43.299
But the main problem is if you now have all of these edge devices,

02:20:43.519 --> 02:20:46.299
if they all send their computation more and more to the cloud,

02:20:46.760 --> 02:20:48.299
you're going to reach certain limitations.

02:20:48.719 --> 02:20:50.659
The limitations have to do with network bandwidth.

02:20:51.239 --> 02:20:54.699
There's so much network that you have until you reach the limits

02:20:54.699 --> 02:20:56.699
of how much you can send over the network.

02:20:57.340 --> 02:21:00.340
So then people started exploring with a different kind of system,

02:21:00.340 --> 02:21:04.460
especially for, let's say, edge devices that collaborate together.

02:21:04.920 --> 02:21:07.760
Let's say, you know, edge devices that might reside in a factory,

02:21:08.460 --> 02:21:11.979
you know, sensors and actuators in a factory of smart cities

02:21:11.979 --> 02:21:14.639
or even inside a normal organization.

02:21:14.819 --> 02:21:17.920
So people started building edge computing systems.

02:21:18.360 --> 02:21:22.600
So these are, again, computer clusters that sit in between the edge devices

02:21:22.600 --> 02:21:25.200
and the cloud computing systems.

02:21:25.920 --> 02:21:27.620
And they can serve various purposes.

02:21:27.620 --> 02:21:29.860
They can implement various kinds of services.

02:21:30.340 --> 02:21:39.059
In reality, when DePaul does that, so for example, DePaul makes use of the cloud systems provided by Microsoft to manage email and accounts.

02:21:39.239 --> 02:21:44.360
But for example, when they record the classrooms and everything, these are all stored on a local server.

02:21:45.000 --> 02:21:54.299
And in order to have very good latency, they probably have an edge computer system in the Loop campus and in another edge computer system in the Lingampar campus.

02:21:54.860 --> 02:22:00.180
And they transfer data locally from the classrooms to their servers and they process them, after which they upload them to the cloud.

02:22:00.340 --> 02:22:04.620
That's why it takes a little bit of time for the recording to be available on B2L.

02:22:04.700 --> 02:22:06.960
It's not available immediately after we finish the lecture.

02:22:07.420 --> 02:22:09.520
It usually takes about two hours to be uploaded.

02:22:10.280 --> 02:22:17.620
So again, these are examples on how people can set up these edge, let's say, computer systems.

02:22:17.620 --> 02:22:21.219
Sometimes they call them edge networks that connects to different kinds of networks.

02:22:21.840 --> 02:22:25.340
What's very important to know, and of course there are more details over here,

02:22:25.340 --> 02:22:38.860
What's very important to know is that understanding how to build and how to manage these edge, let's say, systems, but also edge computing systems, it's still an open problem.

02:22:39.319 --> 02:22:47.780
There's still a lot of companies that are working on building frameworks and platforms for automatically managing edge devices and edge computing systems.

02:22:48.399 --> 02:22:53.239
There's still a lot of work that is done in the area of resource allocation, service placement, edge selection.

02:22:53.239 --> 02:22:55.699
so if you are at some point interested

02:22:55.699 --> 02:22:57.799
and you want to really decide

02:22:57.799 --> 02:23:00.000
hey I want to become a distributed systems engineer

02:23:00.000 --> 02:23:01.739
I would search for companies that are

02:23:01.739 --> 02:23:03.539
working in this area and I would

02:23:03.539 --> 02:23:05.639
do more reading in this area to learn

02:23:05.639 --> 02:23:07.760
what people are doing to see

02:23:07.760 --> 02:23:09.619
if you can contribute to their system

02:23:09.619 --> 02:23:11.699
and help them build a framework that can manage

02:23:11.699 --> 02:23:13.619
all of these devices. Unfortunately

02:23:13.619 --> 02:23:15.600
with cloud computing systems they're easier

02:23:15.600 --> 02:23:17.559
to manage because you have a uniform

02:23:17.559 --> 02:23:19.600
infrastructure that you can build, you know what

02:23:19.600 --> 02:23:21.760
goes on there, it's controlled by one organization

02:23:21.760 --> 02:23:26.680
for edge devices, it's harder, and for edge computing systems, it's harder as well,

02:23:26.739 --> 02:23:29.139
because they can change from one application to the other.

02:23:29.540 --> 02:23:31.420
They might use different services, right?

02:23:31.420 --> 02:23:35.239
So it might become a little bit harder to manage and to orchestrate

02:23:35.239 --> 02:23:38.159
than cloud computing systems.

02:23:38.239 --> 02:23:41.579
So there's still a lot of work that people are doing here,

02:23:41.659 --> 02:23:44.280
and there's still a lot of open-ended questions on how do we do things,

