and you know it's all great

02:24:12.880 --> 02:24:15.440
but I think it's important for us to just understand them

02:24:15.440 --> 02:24:17.320
at least in terms of computer architecture

02:24:17.319 --> 02:24:20.340
what made them different than other computer systems.

02:24:21.279 --> 02:24:27.799
So what blockchain systems try to solve is the problem of how do we manage,

02:24:28.020 --> 02:24:34.159
let's say, a connection of, let's say, transactions in a system.

02:24:34.379 --> 02:24:37.260
Transaction means I'm doing certain kinds of operations in the system.

02:24:37.619 --> 02:24:43.860
How do we keep a sequence of those transactions consistent across multiple computers

02:24:43.860 --> 02:24:45.739
where we cannot trust the computers?

02:24:45.739 --> 02:24:49.079
and there's no one central entity that we can trust.

02:24:50.039 --> 02:24:53.159
Okay, so in order to do this,

02:24:53.199 --> 02:24:55.039
it means that we had to design a system.

02:24:55.659 --> 02:24:58.139
It's basically a peer-to-peer system.

02:24:58.280 --> 02:24:59.520
It's a system where everyone can act

02:24:59.520 --> 02:25:00.940
with both its clients and servers.

02:25:01.719 --> 02:25:04.619
And the actual roles that we have there in the system

02:25:04.619 --> 02:25:06.699
is we have participants in the systems

02:25:06.699 --> 02:25:10.420
and then systems that maintain the blockchain network.

02:25:11.500 --> 02:25:13.420
And what the participants can do

02:25:13.420 --> 02:25:16.760
is they can perform transactions inside the system.

02:25:16.940 --> 02:25:19.079
Whenever a participant performs a transaction,

02:25:19.239 --> 02:25:22.500
it broadcasts a transaction to all of the possible nodes

02:25:22.500 --> 02:25:23.700
that are connected to the system.

02:25:24.059 --> 02:25:25.579
So whatever you do, for example,

02:25:25.960 --> 02:25:28.100
a Bitcoin transaction is a broadcast.

02:25:28.299 --> 02:25:30.600
It happens that, hey, I'm doing this transaction right now.

02:25:30.920 --> 02:25:33.319
And multiple compute nodes can capture that transaction.

02:25:33.860 --> 02:25:36.200
Now, of course, there's also the notion of validate R-NOS.

02:25:36.280 --> 02:25:38.460
So these are nodes that collect all of the transactions

02:25:38.460 --> 02:25:40.960
and they try to put them in a list of transactions

02:25:40.960 --> 02:25:42.719
or a chain of transactions.

02:25:42.719 --> 02:25:44.379
Well, they don't put it in a chain.

02:25:44.539 --> 02:25:46.779
They put, actually, the transactions inside one block.

02:25:47.219 --> 02:25:49.959
And then what they try to do is they try to append the block

02:25:49.959 --> 02:25:52.659
to an existing chain of blocks of transactions.

02:25:52.799 --> 02:25:54.059
But this needs to be consistent.

02:25:54.479 --> 02:25:57.819
In order to be consistent, it means that the other nodes have to trust

02:25:57.819 --> 02:26:00.319
that the operation was done correctly.

02:26:00.639 --> 02:26:04.699
The way that they do this is by collecting all of the blocks,

02:26:04.859 --> 02:26:08.959
validating the transactions, and then solving a very complex puzzle

02:26:08.959 --> 02:26:13.119
in order to decide which of the nodes is allowed to append to the blockchain.

02:26:13.259 --> 02:26:15.619
Because the computational puzzle is very hard,

02:26:15.799 --> 02:26:17.379
only one of the machines can do it.

02:26:17.819 --> 02:26:20.199
And because it costs a lot of resources to do it,

02:26:20.239 --> 02:26:21.219
we then believe the machines.

02:26:21.219 --> 02:26:24.419
And then we can easily validate the results of that puzzle.

02:26:24.559 --> 02:26:30.599
So let's go over the system and look at a little bit of the properties

02:26:30.599 --> 02:26:35.439
and let's see what it would take to create the system

02:26:35.440 --> 02:26:39.159
and to move from, let's say, a centralized solution to a distributive solution

02:26:39.159 --> 02:26:40.680
and to a decentralized solution.

02:26:41.180 --> 02:26:42.860
So again, some important properties.

02:26:44.260 --> 02:26:50.140
The blocks organized in the blockchain are organized in an unforgeable append-only chain.

02:26:50.340 --> 02:26:53.600
So once you append the block to the chain and expand the chain,

02:26:53.659 --> 02:26:55.460
the chain cannot be modified anymore.

02:26:55.720 --> 02:26:56.960
So it's append-only.

02:26:57.260 --> 02:26:59.960
So it keeps, again, track of the history of all of the transactions.

02:27:01.420 --> 02:27:04.940
So since the blockchain cannot be modified anymore, so it's immutable,

02:27:05.440 --> 02:27:08.079
You can massively replicate the blockchain.

02:27:08.280 --> 02:27:11.739
So anyone can go over the blockchain and validate all of the transactions.

02:27:12.200 --> 02:27:17.220
That's how they make sure that, you know, nobody lies whenever they do transactions with Bitcoins.

02:27:17.399 --> 02:27:18.940
Or if they lie, they can be detected.

02:27:19.340 --> 02:27:20.960
And then the transaction can be cancelled.

02:27:22.180 --> 02:27:22.620
Right.

02:27:22.760 --> 02:27:26.500
But the problem is, you know, how do you append a block to a blockchain?

02:27:27.020 --> 02:27:30.460
So this can be implemented, of course, using a centralized solution.

02:27:30.620 --> 02:27:31.700
That would be the easiest way.

02:27:31.819 --> 02:27:31.980
Right.

02:27:32.040 --> 02:27:34.380
A centralized solution means you have a central computer.

02:27:34.379 --> 02:27:38.219
that it's responsible with attaching the blocks to the blockchain.

02:27:38.959 --> 02:27:42.359
So if anyone wants to do transactions,

02:27:42.559 --> 02:27:45.779
they add them to the block and then send the block to the centralized entity.

02:27:46.119 --> 02:27:48.279
The centralized entity can validate the transaction,

02:27:48.420 --> 02:27:49.579
make sure that everything consists,

02:27:49.699 --> 02:27:52.139
and make sure that nobody modified the blockchain.

02:27:52.539 --> 02:27:53.279
Everything is good.

02:27:53.679 --> 02:27:54.259
You move both.

02:27:54.799 --> 02:27:57.859
But again, the problem is this is not a decentralized solution.

02:27:57.859 --> 02:28:01.119
And the question is if somebody compromises the centralized system,

02:28:01.120 --> 02:28:06.880
right we cannot trust them as we cannot trust the bank when they developed

02:28:06.880 --> 02:28:12.400
critical practices now we can also implement a distributed solution so

02:28:12.400 --> 02:28:17.720
distributed solutions we select a subset of the nodes to be responsible with

02:28:17.720 --> 02:28:24.579
deciding right if certain you know blockchain is correct or not and then

02:28:24.579 --> 02:28:26.620
to then decide if we want to

02:28:26.620 --> 02:28:27.840
append a block to the blockchain.

02:28:30.299 --> 02:28:32.579
So these systems, they can, you know, build their

02:28:32.579 --> 02:28:34.379
own consensus there or algorithms

02:28:34.379 --> 02:28:36.520
to determine which one is correct or which

02:28:36.520 --> 02:28:38.539
one is true. The only problem

02:28:38.539 --> 02:28:40.479
with this is that the more nodes

02:28:40.479 --> 02:28:42.340
you add, the more communication

02:28:42.340 --> 02:28:44.239
we have to do between them,

02:28:44.639 --> 02:28:45.979
then it becomes

02:28:45.979 --> 02:28:52.440
from a scalability point of view, very hard to achieve.

02:28:53.399 --> 02:28:57.100
Now, if you have a small number of nodes, this still becomes dangerous because they

02:28:57.100 --> 02:29:00.020
can get compromised and you cannot trust the system, right?

02:29:00.020 --> 02:29:01.319
So you want to have a larger number.

02:29:02.140 --> 02:29:05.640
So then the solution was to build a decent solution.

02:29:06.960 --> 02:29:11.680
Again, it's called a permissionless solution where the nodes can play either the role of

02:29:11.680 --> 02:29:14.579
participants or validators or both at the same time.

02:29:15.399 --> 02:29:24.719
Now, the participants, what they do is they collectively engage in a distributed leader election.

02:29:25.059 --> 02:29:30.819
So basically, what they try to do is whenever they have a block that is ready to be appended to the blockchain,

02:29:31.680 --> 02:29:37.479
you will allow only one of the compute nodes to actually append that block to the blockchain.

02:29:37.479 --> 02:29:49.340
And in order to choose that leader, they use some sort of election system that needs to be fair, robust, secure, which is far from, let's say, trivial.

02:29:49.979 --> 02:29:58.199
Now, we will talk more about how they implement this leader election a little bit later when we talk about, I think, coordination in distributed systems.

02:29:58.319 --> 02:30:06.359
When we look at the actual algorithm and how they use, let's say, hash functions to determine who is the winner of this leader election.

02:30:07.060 --> 02:30:10.000
But again, this allows now only one of the systems

02:30:10.000 --> 02:30:11.380
to add data to the blockchain.

02:30:12.120 --> 02:30:15.960
Whenever that, let's say, a participant

02:30:15.960 --> 02:30:18.780
wins the leader election, it can be validated

02:30:18.780 --> 02:30:21.280
that it was the one that won the election

02:30:21.280 --> 02:30:22.480
and then they can append the data.

02:30:22.960 --> 02:30:24.840
And the algorithm is designed in a way

02:30:24.840 --> 02:30:27.980
where somebody who appended, let's say,

02:30:28.000 --> 02:30:30.760
information to the data will have a very hard time

02:30:30.760 --> 02:30:32.480
of pinning information again very soon.

02:30:33.100 --> 02:30:36.079
So other systems, you know, will be, let's say,

02:30:36.079 --> 02:30:41.739
probabilistically selected randomly out of the entire system in order to

02:30:41.739 --> 02:30:47.440
append data and update the blockchain. The blockchain again is then massively

02:30:47.440 --> 02:30:51.559
distributed across all of the other nodes. Since the blockchain itself is

02:30:51.559 --> 02:30:55.680
immutable, it means that everyone can restart a leader election whenever they

