at inserting the programs in the computers to execute.

02:40:18.719 --> 02:40:20.859
There used to be a lot of physical involvement

02:40:20.859 --> 02:40:22.260
whenever they loaded a program.

02:40:23.020 --> 02:40:24.559
So then they started looking at,

02:40:24.680 --> 02:40:26.340
okay, how can we design a system

02:40:26.340 --> 02:40:28.940
where the people can work on the system

02:40:28.940 --> 02:40:32.120
at the same time, write the programs, right,

02:40:32.120 --> 02:40:34.440
and then have the system execute the programs

02:40:34.440 --> 02:40:35.520
one after the other.

02:40:37.220 --> 02:40:40.260
So this gave birth to some early systems

02:40:40.260 --> 02:40:42.600
called batch processing systems.

02:40:43.220 --> 02:40:46.020
This is where that notion of a batch script

02:40:46.020 --> 02:40:47.600
in Windows comes from, actually.

02:40:48.300 --> 02:40:51.600
So batch processing.

02:40:51.600 --> 02:40:59.700
Now batch processing systems use the notion of jobs

02:40:59.700 --> 02:41:02.200
So they didn't have the notion of processing

02:41:02.200 --> 02:41:05.079
So each program would run as a job

02:41:05.079 --> 02:41:10.800
And people started building a very basic operating system

02:41:10.800 --> 02:41:12.540
Called the batch processing system

02:41:12.540 --> 02:41:15.340
That allowed users to actually load programs

02:41:15.340 --> 02:41:16.960
While other programs were running

02:41:16.960 --> 02:41:19.840
Now the only difference is that the batch processing system

02:41:19.840 --> 02:41:22.819
only allowed one program to execute at a time.

02:41:23.360 --> 02:41:26.899
So this meant that if the program required user input,

02:41:27.239 --> 02:41:31.100
the whole system would block until the user came

02:41:31.100 --> 02:41:33.719
and, you know, inputted the data, right?

02:41:33.739 --> 02:41:36.239
So this was not a very interactive system.

02:41:36.239 --> 02:41:40.920
And it only ran one job, one process, let's say, at a time.

02:41:41.960 --> 02:41:45.159
Now, sometimes, right, people started inserting errors

02:41:45.159 --> 02:41:46.239
in their programs, right?

02:41:46.280 --> 02:41:47.299
The programs weren't perfect.

02:41:47.440 --> 02:41:49.440
So sometimes, you know, the program would crash.

02:41:49.840 --> 02:41:52.139
which means that the entire system will crash.

02:41:52.139 --> 02:41:57.920
So we needed to have some sort of mechanism that allowed us to run multiple processes,

02:41:58.500 --> 02:42:00.299
multiple jobs in a safe way.

02:42:00.620 --> 02:42:05.000
And now we see, finally, the emergence of an operating system.

02:42:05.200 --> 02:42:09.000
So an operating system not only allows you to run jobs,

02:42:09.040 --> 02:42:11.500
but also allows you to run these jobs in a safe way,

02:42:11.600 --> 02:42:14.960
where if one job fails, the entire system still runs.

02:42:15.600 --> 02:42:18.819
So it has good fault tolerance from a programming perspective, let's say.

02:42:19.840 --> 02:42:38.360
And at the same time, the computers became so fast that if we were to execute these programs one at a time, it wouldn't be very efficient, especially if the programs required input or especially if the programs required to access data from slower devices.

02:42:39.079 --> 02:42:45.760
As computers became faster, we started creating, again, this memory hierarchy as well.

02:42:45.760 --> 02:42:52.100
So at the beginning, the processors, they were running as fast as the memory where you store the data, either volatile or persist.

02:42:52.860 --> 02:43:03.840
But as technology became more advanced, it allows us to create faster processors, but with smaller amounts of registers or local memory,

02:43:04.219 --> 02:43:09.479
which then we started creating this hierarchy of different memories to allow for greater capacity,

02:43:09.479 --> 02:43:14.940
but at the cost of having to bring that data throughout that memory hierarchy back to the processor.

02:43:16.300 --> 02:43:23.760
So this meant that now there were certain processes or certain jobs that were designed in a way where they were waiting for data to come from this.

02:43:24.260 --> 02:43:24.460
Right?

02:43:24.520 --> 02:43:32.500
And it would be very inefficient if those processes just stayed on the processor while they were waiting for data to come.

02:43:32.500 --> 02:43:38.579
It might be more efficient to allow another process to run on the processor while the process is waiting for data.

02:43:39.219 --> 02:43:45.180
So step by step, we started going into the area of preemptive scheduling.

02:43:45.180 --> 02:43:50.040
So again, this problem of what process runs on the processor is called the scheduling problem.

02:43:50.720 --> 02:43:54.760
And preemptive scheduling means that, and there are two ways to do scheduling.

02:43:55.000 --> 02:43:57.659
There's cooperative scheduling and preemptive scheduling.

02:43:58.360 --> 02:44:04.720
Cooperative means that the processes themselves, for example, when they detect that I have to wait for some data,

02:44:05.200 --> 02:44:08.300
will go to sleep by themselves and will allow another process to run.

02:44:08.299 --> 02:44:11.459
preemptively means that the operating system

02:44:11.459 --> 02:44:13.459
will actually interrupt the processes

02:44:13.459 --> 02:44:15.359
and will only allow them to run

02:44:15.359 --> 02:44:16.819
for a very small amount of time,

02:44:16.819 --> 02:44:18.939
typically measured in microseconds,

02:44:19.599 --> 02:44:22.439
depends on nanoseconds as well,

02:44:22.559 --> 02:44:23.579
hundreds of nanoseconds,

02:44:23.679 --> 02:44:25.340
depends on how fast the processor is.

02:44:26.199 --> 02:44:28.119
So modern-day operating systems

02:44:28.119 --> 02:44:29.139
are designing a way

02:44:29.139 --> 02:44:30.539
where they allow the processes

02:44:30.539 --> 02:44:32.439
to run for a very small amount of time.

02:44:32.819 --> 02:44:33.679
I'm talking about single,

02:44:34.039 --> 02:44:34.979
four processors here,

02:44:35.079 --> 02:44:36.319
like one process at a time,

02:44:36.639 --> 02:44:37.699
for a very small amount,

02:44:37.700 --> 02:44:40.579
and then it takes that process out of the processor

02:44:40.579 --> 02:44:43.020
and it brings another process on the processor

02:44:43.020 --> 02:44:45.720
and it makes this transition so fast

02:44:45.720 --> 02:44:48.159
that even on a single processor computer,

02:44:48.380 --> 02:44:51.900
for us it appears as if the system is executing processes

02:44:51.900 --> 02:44:53.440
at the same time.

02:44:53.720 --> 02:44:55.159
But they're not really at the same time.

02:44:55.260 --> 02:44:56.900
Only one process at a time gets executed,

02:44:57.040 --> 02:45:00.340
but very small parts and this quick exchange of processes

02:45:00.340 --> 02:45:03.120
happens so fast that we don't see those changes.

02:45:03.440 --> 02:45:06.540
We know that a monitor, for example,

02:45:06.540 --> 02:45:10.800
refresh rate for a monitor is about 60 Hz.

02:45:11.360 --> 02:45:13.940
Because that's what the human eye can

02:45:13.940 --> 02:45:17.580
detect in terms of changes. Now I know you're playing video games,

02:45:17.660 --> 02:45:21.760
maybe 120 Hz, 144, 200. Maybe if your eye

02:45:21.760 --> 02:45:25.800
is really that trained and the latency allows you to be a better

02:45:25.800 --> 02:45:28.460
player, then it makes sense. But for the rest of us,

02:45:28.720 --> 02:45:33.520
60 Hz is enough. So the computers, they can do way more

02:45:33.520 --> 02:45:35.860
computation than 60 Hz. If you remember,

02:45:36.720 --> 02:45:37.159
Right.

02:45:37.200 --> 02:45:41.040
A processor can execute the length of the structures per second.

02:45:41.040 --> 02:45:45.480
So it works at 4 GHz, let's say, speed or 3 GHz speed.

02:45:45.840 --> 02:45:50.400
So it can do way more mini-computation, which means it can schedule way more mini-processes

02:45:50.400 --> 02:45:51.620
very fast on the processor.

02:45:52.140 --> 02:45:56.600
And that way, it gives us the impression that multiple things are happening in the system.

02:45:56.880 --> 02:46:03.319
So this whole idea of running these processes and interchanging them on the processor and

02:46:03.319 --> 02:46:07.219
giving them a small amount of time to run, is called multiprocessing, like you said.

02:46:07.559 --> 02:46:09.659
I want to be able to run multiple processes.

02:46:09.819 --> 02:46:12.619
It gives the impression that they're running simultaneously,

02:46:12.799 --> 02:46:14.579
but they're actually not running simultaneously.

02:46:14.979 --> 02:46:17.079
So only one process at a time exists.

02:46:18.940 --> 02:46:19.199
All right.

02:46:19.239 --> 02:46:22.539
Now, we see this is very different from multithreading,

02:46:22.680 --> 02:46:24.579
which refers to something else.

02:46:25.100 --> 02:46:29.379
It refers to how do we organize one single application to multiple components.

02:46:30.379 --> 02:46:35.739
So one of the problems that people started facing once they started building more advanced applications

02:46:35.739 --> 02:46:42.199
is they noticed that one application might do multiple things at the same time.

02:46:42.199 --> 02:46:47.939
Let's take the example of a word processor application, like Microsoft Word in the early days.

02:46:48.559 --> 02:46:50.679
At the beginning, the word processor was simple.

02:46:50.799 --> 02:46:51.939
It was kind of like Notepad.

02:46:52.179 --> 02:46:55.000
You just open up, and you write, and read data, and that's it.

02:46:55.139 --> 02:46:56.299
It doesn't do anything fancy.

02:46:56.899 --> 02:46:59.279
But later on, people wanted to have more capabilities.

02:46:59.659 --> 02:47:02.099
They wanted to be able to do spell checking, for example.

02:47:02.979 --> 02:47:07.159
Right now, the way spell checking was implemented back in the day,

02:47:07.159 --> 02:47:09.899
in the early days of Microsoft Word, is you had to press a button,

02:47:10.840 --> 02:47:12.439
which will then tell your application,

02:47:12.599 --> 02:47:16.420
hey, scan the entire document, identify all of the words that are incorrect,

02:47:16.840 --> 02:47:21.979
put that red underscore under it, or line under it,

02:47:22.340 --> 02:47:26.039
but you weren't able to modify the document while that process was running.

02:47:26.299 --> 02:47:29.039
right so your entire application was now stuck

02:47:29.039 --> 02:47:30.859
and you had to wait until it was done before

02:47:30.859 --> 02:47:31.779
it read more input

02:47:31.779 --> 02:47:34.719
maybe you wanted to

02:47:34.719 --> 02:47:35.139
yes

